  "mission": "Scale your prompts into plans and your plans into templates — entering the plan-and-build phase of the agentic software development lifecycle. With just three tactics (Stop Coding, Adopt Your Agent's Perspective, Template Your Engineering) you get the Pareto 80/20 of agentic engineering, and you unlock the ability to not just augment but automate the entire SDLC with agents shipping work on your behalf. 'Those who plan the future tend to create it. Success is planned.'",
  "tactic": {
    "name": "Template Your Engineering",
    "one_liner": "Encode your engineering workflows and best practices into reusable templates — meta-prompts that generate plans — so agents can ship whole classes of work (chores, bugs, features) with your team's standards, over and over, on any size codebase.",
    "why_uncomfortable": "Three separate discomforts. First, planning was the engineer's sacred craft — the thing you did BECAUSE you'd stopped typing code — and this tactic hands even the plan-writing to the agent ('let our agents write the first and sometimes final draft of our plans'). Second, it demands you throw away the conversation: fresh, memory-less agent instances for every phase instead of one long omniscient session — Dan pre-acknowledges it on camera: 'I know this looks weird. I know it might feel uncomfortable. We don't see our agent.' Third, templates are an upfront investment that produces zero code on day one; you're paying engineering time to encode process, trusting the payoff arrives across hundreds of agent executions."
