48:      <header class="hero">
49:        <span class="hero__chapter">Tactic #1 · Stop Coding</span>
50:        <h1>Hello Agentic Coding</h1>
51:        <p class="hero__dek">The decisive move is not a better code-generation prompt. It is turning a prompt into a reliable engineering workflow—and turning the agent into a component you can call.</p>
52:        <ul class="hero__meta" aria-label="Chapter details">
58:        <p class="hero__promise"><strong>Mastery promise.</strong> By the end, you will be able to diagnose agentic work with the Core Four, explain the exact difference between AI coding and agentic coding, reproduce Dan’s interactive and headless demos, and teach why “replace yourself” is a leverage strategy rather than a surrender.</p>
59:        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate generated-plate--hero">
65:      <div class="mobile-toc">
85:      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
86:        <span class="section-number">00</span>
87:        <h2 class="section-title">Begin with the failure, not the slogan</h2>
88:        <p class="section-lead">You ask an AI to write a function. It gives you plausible code. You paste it into the repository, discover that it does not fit, repair two imports, run the tests, fix a test, create a branch, make the commit, and explain the result in Slack. The AI helped with one narrow slice. You still carried the workflow.</p>
94:        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
95:          <p class="callout__label">The chapter’s one-sentence thesis</p>
99:        <h3>What you are training</h3>
100:        <ul class="learning-objectives">
107:        <h3>How to use this chapter</h3>
110:        <div class="callout callout--warning">
111:          <p class="callout__label">Prime directive for every exercise</p>
116:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-role">
117:        <span class="section-number">01</span>
118:        <h2 class="section-title">A new role: the Agentic Engineer</h2>
119:        <p class="section-lead">The title matters only if the work changes. “Agentic Engineer” is not a badge for someone who uses AI often; it names an engineer who builds systems able to take engineering actions.</p>
121:        <div class="move move--hook">
122:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
123:          <h3>The prompt expert who is still the workflow engine</h3>
128:        <div class="move move--picture">
129:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the two-phase map</p>
130:          <h3>Move the boundary of delegation</h3>
131:          <div class="comparison" aria-label="AI coding and agentic coding roles compared">
132:            <article class="comparison__card">
133:              <div class="comparison__head"><h3>Phase 1 · AI coding</h3><p>The agent produces code; the human carries the workflow.</p></div>
134:              <div class="comparison__body">
135:                <div class="workflow workflow--stacked">
136:                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>context → prompt</code></div>
137:                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Agent</strong><code>generate code</code></div>
138:                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>write → run → fix → commit → report</code></div>
142:            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic">
143:              <div class="comparison__head"><h3>Phase 2 · Agentic coding</h3><p>The human specifies and reviews; the agent carries the workflow.</p></div>
144:              <div class="comparison__body">
145:                <div class="workflow workflow--stacked">
146:                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>goal + constraints + checks</code></div>
147:                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Agent</strong><code>branch → write → run → commit → report</code></div>
148:                  <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human</strong><code>review outcome + improve loop</code></div>
156:        <div class="move move--story">
157:          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
158:          <h3>A 2023 bet becomes a visible phase change</h3>
160:          <figure class="frame-card">
162:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">1:55</span>Dan places AI coding and agentic coding on a two-phase timeline. Keep this scaffold visible: phase two is a superset, not a rejection, of phase one.</figcaption>
167:        <div class="move move--name">
168:          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
169:          <h3>Phase-two Agentic Engineer</h3>
170:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
171:            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p>
178:        <div class="move move--example">
179:          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
180:          <h3>The repository describes a worker, not a product</h3>
182:          <div class="file-map">
183:            <div class="file-map__item"><code>README.md</code><p>Holds the phase-one and phase-two prompts plus the three launcher commands.</p></div>
184:            <div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/settings.json</code><p>Declares the tools the agent may use: three file tools, UV, four Git operations, and WebSearch.</p></div>
185:            <div class="file-map__item"><code>programmable/prompt.md</code><p>Packages a self-contained branch → create → run → commit → report workflow.</p></div>
186:            <div class="file-map__item"><code>programmable/*.{sh,py,ts}</code><p>Calls the same prompt from three host languages, proving the agent is a component.</p></div>
187:            <div class="file-map__item"><code>.env.sample</code><p>Contains the API-key placeholder used by the headless billing path.</p></div>
188:            <div class="file-map__item"><code>main_*.py</code><p><strong>Not source files at the start.</strong> They are runtime artifacts created by the agent.</p></div>
193:        <div class="move move--try">
194:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — 3 minute drill</p>
195:          <h3>Classify the work by who carries the transitions</h3>
205:        <div class="move move--fail">
206:          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
207:          <h3>Failure: a title change without a delegation change</h3>
212:        <div class="move move--current">
213:          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
214:          <h3>The ceiling is higher; the primitive is unchanged</h3>
215:          <aside class="current-practice">
216:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
218:            <p class="current-practice__source">Forward pointer: later TAC lessons scale the seed planted here.</p>
222:        <div class="move move--check">
223:          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
224:          <h3>Close the section before revealing answers</h3>
225:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for the Agentic Engineer role">
226:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What behavior, not job title, defines Dan’s Agentic Engineer?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Building systems that can take actions as engineers can—such as branching, writing, running, committing, and reporting.</p></details></div>
227:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is an excellent one-shot code prompt still phase-one shaped?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the output is code while the human still carries the engineering workflow around it. Quality does not change who performs the transitions.</p></details></div>
228:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is the smallest observable proof that your role has moved?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A system completes at least one meaningful multi-action chain without the human relaying intermediate results, then returns evidence for review.</p></details></div>
232:        <div class="move move--teach">
233:          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
234:          <div class="teach-back">
236:            <p class="fine">Pass if your explanation includes actions, a repeatable system, and a report—not merely “AI does more.”</p>
241:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-mission">
242:        <span class="section-number">02</span>
243:        <h2 class="section-title">The mission: command compute through leverage</h2>
244:        <p class="section-lead">The tactic is emotionally difficult because it asks you to retire a visible skill. The mission supplies the reason: move up the stack from producing keystrokes to designing systems that produce outcomes.</p>
246:        <div class="move move--hook">
247:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
248:          <h3>The faster typist who cannot escape throughput</h3>
253:        <div class="move move--picture">
254:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — attention versus repeatability</p>
255:          <h3>Effort is linear; a reusable system bends the curve</h3>
256:          <figure class="diagram">
257:            <div class="diagram__canvas">
278:        <div class="move move--story">
279:          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
280:          <h3>He names the enemy, then changes the identity</h3>
282:          <div class="frame-grid">
283:            <figure class="frame-card">
285:              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">2:04</span>The mission supplies the criterion by which later tactics are judged: do they increase your leverage and make your contribution harder to substitute?</figcaption>
287:            <figure class="frame-card">
289:              <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">2:42</span>The identity shift has a name: commander of compute. Your work moves from producing the artifact to aiming the machinery.</figcaption>
295:        <div class="move move--name">
296:          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
297:          <h3>Commander of compute</h3>
298:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
299:            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p>
306:        <div class="move move--example">
307:          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
308:          <h3>Where the leverage lives in <code>programmable/</code></h3>
323:        <div class="move move--try">
324:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — leverage ledger</p>
325:          <h3>Measure attention, not impressiveness</h3>
336:        <div class="move move--fail">
337:          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
338:          <h3>Failure: confusing more output with more leverage</h3>
343:        <div class="move move--current">
344:          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
345:          <h3>Period detail changes; leverage does not</h3>
346:          <aside class="current-practice">
347:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
349:            <p class="current-practice__source">Use today’s tool, but grade it with the same question: how much reliable outcome does one unit of attention purchase?</p>
353:        <div class="move move--check">
354:          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
355:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for leverage and mission">
356:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Why does Dan say the “10x” number is arbitrary?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Because the multiplier comes from the leverage point you find. It could be 2x, 10x, or 100x; it does not come from a fixed amount of greater effort.</p></details></div>
357:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What makes the programmable demo leverage rather than just automation theater?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The workflow can be recreated repeatedly from a known state with low additional human attention, and it returns evidence through the report.</p></details></div>
358:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does a commander of compute decide?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The target, context, constraints, bounded tools, success evidence, and timing of a system that performs engineering work.</p></details></div>
362:        <div class="move move--teach">
363:          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
364:          <div class="teach-back">
366:            <p class="fine">Pass if you mention repetition, marginal attention, evidence, and a judgment boundary.</p>
371:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-stop">
372:        <span class="section-number">03</span>
373:        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
374:        <p class="section-lead">The provocation is precise: stop <em>hand-typing</em> application code. Keep generating code. Keep understanding code. Move your scarce cycles to planning, reviewing, and building closed loops.</p>
376:        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate">
381:        <div class="move move--hook">
382:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
383:          <h3>The ninety-percent trap</h3>
389:        <div class="move move--picture">
390:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — reallocate the engineering cycle</p>
391:          <h3>Do not remove engineering; move it around the code</h3>
392:          <figure class="diagram">
393:            <div class="diagram__canvas">
422:        <div class="move move--story">
423:          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
424:          <h3>The tactic is said twice because the easy interpretation is wrong</h3>
426:          <figure class="frame-card">
428:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:54</span>The headline is intentionally uncomfortable. Read the object of the verb carefully: stop personally <em>typing</em> the code, not producing or understanding it.</figcaption>
433:        <div class="move move--name">
434:          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
435:          <h3>Stop coding ≠ stop engineering</h3>
436:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
437:            <p class="callout__label">Exact distinction</p>
449:        <div class="move move--example">
450:          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — walk the course codebase</p>
451:          <h3>Zero application-code keystrokes in TAC-1</h3>
457:          <aside class="callout callout--why">
458:            <p class="callout__label">Why the constraint matters</p>
463:        <div class="move move--try">
464:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — repair without reclaiming the keyboard</p>
465:          <h3>Use the next imperfection as the exercise</h3>
476:        <div class="move move--fail">
477:          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
478:          <h3>Two opposite misunderstandings</h3>
479:          <div class="comparison">
480:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No more code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> code remains a primary asset that produces features and products. TAC expects you to generate more of it.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> say “stop hand-typing” every time you teach the tactic.</p></div></article>
481:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No need to understand code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> faster generation magnifies the cost of weak review and weak system judgment.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move reading, execution, tests, and diff review into explicit pass criteria.</p></div></article>
486:        <div class="move move--current">
487:          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
488:          <h3>The tactic has a public companion</h3>
489:          <aside class="current-practice">
490:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current-practice video · plan-approved</p>
492:            <p class="current-practice__source">Metadata should be rechecked at publication; the ID is supplied by the locked extract.</p>
494:          <div class="video-cards video-cards--single">
495:            <a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2TIXl2rlA6Q">
496:              <span class="video-card__type">Operationalizes the tactic</span>
499:              <span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span>
504:        <div class="move move--check">
505:          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
506:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for Stop Coding">
507:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What exactly stops when you follow Tactic #1?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Your hand-typing of application code. Code generation and code understanding continue.</p></details></div>
508:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Where do the freed engineering cycles go?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Planning, reviewing, and creating closed-loop structures.</p></details></div>
509:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is fixing the last two lines by hand a course failure even when it is faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It completes the artifact while hiding the loop’s weakness. You lose the chance to improve agent instructions, feedback, and verification for repeatable future work.</p></details></div>
510:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What must increase when agents generate more code?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The quality and systematization of planning, review, validation, and governance.</p></details></div>
514:        <div class="move move--teach">
515:          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
516:          <div class="teach-back">
518:            <p class="fine">Pass if the learner could act differently on their next task.</p>
523:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-core-four">
524:        <span class="section-number">04</span>
525:        <h2 class="section-title">Big Three → Core Four</h2>
526:        <p class="section-lead">The phase change adds one diagnostic dimension: tools. The novelty is not that tools exist. The breakthrough is that a prompt can reliably carry a long chain of tool calls at useful scale and performance.</p>
528:        <figure class="frame-card generated-plate">
533:        <div class="move move--hook">
534:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
535:          <h3>The reroll reflex</h3>
541:        <div class="move move--picture">
542:          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the Core Four visual</p>
543:          <h3>Four interacting primitives, one newly emphasized dimension</h3>
544:          <figure class="diagram">
545:            <div class="diagram__canvas">
546:              <div class="core-four" role="img" aria-label="Core Four: context, model, prompt, and tools, with tools highlighted as the added phase-two dimension">
547:                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Context</b><p>What the model can see: repo, files, settings, current state.</p></div>
548:                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Model</b><p>The reasoning and generation capacity doing the work.</p></div>
549:                <div class="core-four__cell"><b>Prompt</b><p>The outcome, constraints, ordered actions, and report request.</p></div>
550:                <div class="core-four__cell core-four__cell--tools"><b>Tools</b><p>The bounded actions the agent can execute in a reliable chain.</p></div>
551:                <span class="core-four__plus">4</span>
556:          <div class="concept-strip">
557:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Context</b><p>“Can it see what matters?”</p></div>
558:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Model</b><p>“Can it reason through this?”</p></div>
559:            <div class="concept-strip__item"><b>Prompt</b><p>“Does it know the outcome and checks?”</p></div>
560:            <div class="concept-strip__item concept-strip__item--new"><b>Tools</b><p>“Can it take the required actions?”</p></div>
564:        <div class="move move--story">
565:          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s demo</p>
566:          <h3>The full trace makes the fourth circle visible</h3>
568:          <figure class="frame-card">
570:            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">14:05</span>The on-screen Core Four is the lesson’s central abstraction. The highlighted teaching point is reliable long-chain execution, not a claim that tool calling was invented here.</figcaption>
575:        <div class="move move--name">
576:          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
577:          <h3>The Big Three and the Core Four</h3>
578:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
579:            <p class="callout__label">Canonical terms</p>
591:        <div class="move move--example">
592:          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — <code>.claude/settings.json</code></p>
593:          <h3>Read the tool surface before running the prompt</h3>
621:        <div class="move move--try">
622:          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — permissions audit</p>
623:          <h3>Checkpoint zero of the lab begins here</h3>
631:          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Pass:</strong> Read, Write, Edit; checkout, branch, add, commit; push is not allowed; WebSearch is the non-local tool.</div>
634:        <div class="move move--fail">
635:          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
636:          <h3>Failure: treating the Core Four as a memorization diagram</h3>
642:        <div class="move move--current">
643:          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
644:          <h3>Permissions became richer; the audit habit survived</h3>
645:          <aside class="current-practice">
646:            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
648:            <p class="current-practice__source">Teaching rule: learn the visible allowlist first, then add richer gates when the workflow requires them.</p>
652:        <div class="move move--check">
653:          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
654:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Core Four retrieval questions">
655:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the Big Three and the one added dimension.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Context, model, prompt; tools are added to form the Core Four.</p></details></div>
656:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What exact capability makes the tools dimension phase-two significant?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Prompts that can reliably execute long chains of tool calls.</p></details></div>
657:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is Dan’s rebuttal to “tool calling isn’t new”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Novelty is not the deciding issue; the change is useful scale, performance, and reliable long-chain execution.</p></details></div>
658:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Which allowed tool proves the agent can commit but not publish?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>Bash(git commit:*)</code> is allowed, while no <code>git push</code> permission appears.</p></details></div>
662:        <div class="move move--teach">
663:          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
664:          <div class="teach-back">
666:            <p class="fine">Pass if you use evidence rather than “the AI is flaky.”</p>
671:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-ab">
672:        <span class="section-number">05</span>
673:        <h2 class="section-title">Two prompts, one monumental difference</h2>
674:        <p class="section-lead">The chapter’s A/B test holds repository, model, and operator constant. Prompt A creates a file. Prompt B carries an engineering workflow. Run both; the delta is the lesson.</p>
676:        <div class="move move--hook">
677:          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
678:          <h3>“Both made files, so they are basically the same”</h3>
