       <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
-        <span class="section-number">00</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Planning is the engineering surface</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">The weak-agent failure is easy to misread: an implementation wanders, so we blame the model or add another paragraph to the implementation prompt. TAC-3 moves the intervention upstream. Before an agent writes code, encode what a good chore, bug investigation, or feature plan must contain.</p>
-        <p>The lesson arrives with a working natural-language-to-SQL application inherited from TAC-2. It has a Vite client, a FastAPI server, tests, scripts, cached API notes, and a 749-line specification at <code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code>. That last file is not administrative residue. It is evidence: a sufficiently detailed plan can carry the context, decisions, ordering, and validation that a build agent needs. The repository therefore begins with the chapter’s argument already sitting on disk.</p>
-        <p>Dan then makes the process deliberately small and visible. A one-sentence chore becomes a 64-line plan on camera. A different prompt consumes that plan and changes the code. A bug template forces reproduction and root-cause analysis. A feature template produces a plan without an interactive agent on screen. These are not four unrelated tricks. They form one composition chain.</p>
-        <figure class="diagram" aria-labelledby="ladder-caption">
-          <div class="diagram__canvas ladder">
-            <div class="ladder__step"><strong>1 · High-level request</strong><code>one sentence</code></div>
-            <div class="ladder__step"><strong>2 · Meta prompt</strong><code>/chore → plan</code></div>
-            <div class="ladder__step"><strong>3 · HOP</strong><code>/implement &lt;plan&gt;</code></div>
-            <div class="ladder__step"><strong>4 · Programmatic run</strong><code>claude -p → JSONL</code></div>
-          </div>
-          <figcaption id="ladder-caption">The scaling ladder: a small human request enters an encoded planning process; a durable plan feeds a generic executor; the same interface can then be called by another program.</figcaption>
-        </figure>
-        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
-          <p class="callout__label">Two disciplines govern the chapter</p>
-          <p><strong>First:</strong> the planning command creates a file in <code>specs/</code>; it does not implement. <strong>Second:</strong> implementation starts in a new agent instance. If chat memory is required, the plan is not yet a self-contained engineering asset.</p>
-        </aside>
+        <span class="section-number">00</span><h2 class="section-title">Planning fails when it remains a private human activity</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A capable engineer can plan a change, hold exceptions in working memory, explain them to an agent, correct the agent mid-run, and still believe the plan worked. What worked was the engineer’s invisible context. The plan was never tested as an asset.</p>
+        <p>TAC-3 removes that hidden support. Planning becomes a file. The file is created by a class-specific template. A fresh agent receives the file through a tiny universal executor. Validation commands and reproduction steps make the result checkable. Finally, the same planning template runs in programmatic mode without an interactive session. The unit under test is no longer “did this conversation go well?” It is “can this encoded process repeatedly carry a class of work from a small request to evidence?”</p>
+        <p>The course repository makes the argument with unusually tangible artifacts. The 749-line <code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code> already demonstrates that a natural-language plan can carry an entire application. Four short command files then encode how to plan chores, bugs, and features and how to execute any resulting plan. The underlying natural-language SQL application is a workbench. The tactic lives in <code>.claude/commands/</code>.</p>
+        <aside class="callout callout--tactic"><p class="callout__label">The chapter’s one-sentence thesis</p><p>A plan is a prompt scaled up for high impact; a template encodes how your team creates that prompt; a higher-order prompt executes it; fresh agents prove the assets have no hidden conversational dependencies.</p></aside>
         <h3>What you are training</h3>
-        <ul class="learning-objectives">
-          <li><strong>Planning judgment</strong>Tell a durable implementation prompt from a plausible list of tasks.</li>
-          <li><strong>Template literacy</strong>Locate purpose, instructions, scope, output format, and parameter in real command files.</li>
-          <li><strong>Composition</strong>Explain why a meta prompt produces a plan while a HOP consumes one.</li>
-          <li><strong>Context discipline</strong>Separate phases so success does not depend on invisible conversational state.</li>
-          <li><strong>Verification</strong>Grade zero-regression claims, plan-driven tests, diffs, and manual reproduction independently.</li>
-          <li><strong>Operational transfer</strong>Run the exact TAC-3 workflow inside the course repository without hand-writing application code.</li>
-        </ul>
+        <ul class="learning-objectives"><li><strong>Composition</strong>Distinguish a high-level prompt, durable plan, meta-prompt template, and HOP by what each accepts and produces.</li><li><strong>Encoding</strong>Read the five-part template anatomy and explain why chores, bugs, and features need different plan formats.</li><li><strong>Isolation</strong>Separate planning and building across fresh instances so the plan must carry the necessary context.</li><li><strong>Evidence</strong>Grade plans and implementations by artifacts, tests, reproduction, diffs, and reports.</li><li><strong>Transfer</strong>Run a planning template headlessly and recognize its JSONL stream as a machine interface.</li></ul>
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning"><p class="callout__label">Prime directive for the lab</p><p>Type no application code by hand. Prompts and template files are the sanctioned writing surface. If implementation misses, repair the upstream plan or template and re-run through a fresh agent. All required lab work stays inside the TAC-3 course codebase.</p></aside>
       </section>
 
-      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-meta">
-        <span class="section-number">03</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Meta prompts: prompts that build prompts</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">The human supplies a high-level request. The meta prompt researches the repository and turns that small input into the durable plan another agent can execute.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The one-line request that is mistaken for recklessness</h3><p>“Replace all server print statements with proper Python logging” looks far too thin for a repository-wide change. If sent straight to an implementation agent, it may be. The missing idea is that the sentence is not the full prompt. It is an argument passed into a stored planning prompt that already tells the agent where to research, what output to create, and how to validate completeness.</p><p>Weak practice either overloads the one line with every known detail or lets an implementation agent improvise. The meta-prompt pattern keeps the trigger compact while preserving a rigorous planning process.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — one line expands</p><h3>Separate the variable request from the stable method</h3><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Variable</h3><p>Changes every run.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><code>"Replace all server print statements…"</code><p>The work instance enters through <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>.</p></div></article><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Stable</h3><p>Improves across runs.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><code>research + scope + plan format + validation</code><p>The engineering method lives in <code>chore.md</code>.</p></div></article></div><p>This separation is the leverage. The request can be short because the stable planning behavior is stored elsewhere and versioned with the codebase.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — one sentence becomes 64 lines</p><h3>The plan file appears before any application change</h3><p>Dan enters the logging chore. The agent reads the repository, inventories print calls, and writes <code>specs/replace-print-with-logging.md</code>. The on-camera artifact reports 64 lines. The important transformation is not the number; it is the direction: a high-level human request becomes a structured, repository-specific implementation prompt.</p><p>The video agent’s prose summary says 20 print statements, while its own visible inventory and the shipped repository resolve to 18 in <code>server.py</code> plus one in <code>main.py</code>. This discrepancy is useful. Plans are reviewable artifacts, not truth by declaration. Grade the inventory against code.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0585-generated-chore-plan.png" alt="Claude Code showing a 64-line generated logging chore plan and the original one-line request" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">9:45</span>The meta prompt’s output moment: a plan lands in <code>specs/</code>. The visible summary count is not repeated as codebase truth; the repository inventory is 19 total print calls.</figcaption></figure></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term</p><h3>Meta prompt</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s definition</p><p><strong>Meta prompt:</strong> “a prompt that builds a prompt.” In this lesson, the planning template consumes a high-level request and produces a plan.</p></aside><p>Keep it operational: <code>.claude/commands/chore.md</code> is prompt A; the generated file under <code>specs/</code> is prompt B; a later executor consumes prompt B. If no new prompt artifact is produced, this chapter’s specific meta-prompt pattern has not happened.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — trace every boundary</p><h3>Follow <code>/chore</code> from input to artifact</h3><ol><li><strong>Input:</strong> a one-sentence logging chore is inserted at the template’s final <code>## Chore</code> / <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> slot.</li><li><strong>Research:</strong> instructions start at <code>README.md</code>, then constrain attention to <code>app/**</code> and <code>scripts/**</code>.</li><li><strong>Transformation:</strong> the agent resolves placeholders in the fenced Plan Format using repository evidence.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> a named markdown file appears under <code>specs/</code>.</li><li><strong>Stop condition:</strong> application code is unchanged. The plan is reviewed before a separate executor receives it.</li></ol><p>This trace prevents the most common category error: celebrating a strong plan as if the chore were already complete.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — boundary test</p><h3>Identify input, transformer, and output</h3><p>Open <code>tac/tac-3/.claude/commands/chore.md</code>. Point to the exact input slot. Point to the instruction that names the destination. Point to the fenced output schema. Then answer: which file in the application should change when <code>/chore</code> succeeds?</p><p>The correct answer is <em>none</em>. If code changed, the planning boundary was crossed.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: “Great plan—now do it” in the same session</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the planning agent finishes, and the user immediately asks it to implement. The code may be good, but the experiment no longer proves that the plan carries sufficient context. Hidden conversational research can fill gaps in the artifact.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> end the planning phase. Review the plan. Open a new agent and pass only the executor plus plan path. If the second agent struggles, improve the plan or template; do not preserve the invisible dependency.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Modern command features enrich the shell, not the principle</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Command files may support frontmatter, positional arguments, direct file references, and injected command output. Those features can make the research boundary more explicit—for example, by naming allowed tools or argument hints. Teach the shipped plain markdown first. Its five conceptual parts still survive when syntax becomes richer.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Current syntax is volatile; inspect the tool version you publish against before copying upgrade examples.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Meta prompt retrieval questions"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Define a meta prompt and name the lesson example.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A prompt that builds a prompt; <code>/chore</code> uses <code>chore.md</code> to build a plan in <code>specs/</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What are the input and output artifacts?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Input: the one-line chore in <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>. Output: a repository-specific plan such as <code>specs/replace-print-with-logging.md</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why must planning stop before code changes?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>So the plan can be reviewed and then tested as a self-contained prompt in a fresh implementation agent.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Draw the meta-prompt pipeline from memory.</strong> Include the one-line request, <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>, repository research, the Plan Format, and the file under <code>specs/</code>. End by saying what has not happened yet.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your explanation clearly separates plan creation from implementation.</p></div></div>
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="plan-is-prompt">
+        <span class="section-number">01</span><h2 class="section-title">The plan is the prompt</h2><p class="section-lead">Planning stops being pre-work once an agent can execute the artifact directly. The plan becomes the most information-dense prompt in the workflow.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The plan that needs a live narrator</h3><p>Imagine a plan that says “add query history.” The author then spends twenty minutes telling the implementer where history belongs, what existing API shapes to follow, what tests matter, and which UI constraint was non-negotiable. The document did not carry the task. The author did. TAC-3 calls this out by changing the standard: if the plan is the prompt, the plan must contain enough structure for a fresh agent to act without inheriting the planning conversation.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — scale, do not switch mediums</p><h3>One material at five composition levels</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><div class="phase-flow"><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">SMALL</span><h4>High-level prompt</h4><p>“Replace server prints with logging.”</p><ul><li>Names the desired delta</li><li>Deliberately omits implementation detail</li><li>Cheap for a human to write</li></ul></article><div class="phase-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">SCALED</span><h4>Executable plan</h4><p>Inventory, files, ordered tasks, validation commands, notes.</p><ul><li>Carries context</li><li>Can be reviewed before code</li><li>Can boot a fresh implementer</li></ul></article></div></div><figcaption>A plan does not replace the prompt. It expands it until the instruction can carry high-impact work.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the lesson’s thesis appears on screen</p><h3>Great planning is great prompting</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0116-great-planning-is-great-prompting.jpg" alt="Course frame showing the equation Great Planning equals Great Prompting over a laptop" width="1920" height="1080"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">1:56</span>The equation is deliberately blunt. Once the plan is fired into an agent, planning quality and prompting quality are the same operational variable.</figcaption></figure><p class="wide-intro">Dan moves through the vocabulary—plan, specification, PRD—not to establish subtle differences but to collapse them into one function: a prompt scaled up for high impact. Later he calls plans “staging environments for code.” That phrase matters because a staging environment is inspected before production. You can refine the natural-language representation while changes are still cheap, then expand it into code through the implementer.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term + plain English</p><h3>Plans are prompts scaled up</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s equality</p><p><strong>The plan is the prompt.</strong> Plain English: the durable plan file is not a historical explanation of what someone might do. It is the actual instruction another agent will execute.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — evidence already in the repo</p><h3>The application began as a 749-line prompt</h3><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code><p>A 749-line initial plan that built the natural-language SQL application carried into TAC-3.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/client/**</code><p>The Vite/TypeScript interface that lets a user upload sample data and ask questions.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/server/**</code><p>The FastAPI/SQLite implementation with tests, LLM translation, schema inspection, and query execution.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/commands/**</code><p>The next level: files that encode how to produce future plans for different classes of work.</p></div></div><p class="wide-intro">The causal claim is visible: the large plan predates the built application, and TAC-3 keeps the plan in the codebase. You can inspect, version, hand off, and improve it. That durability is the difference between a chat explanation and an engineering artifact.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — three-minute discrimination drill</p><h3>Find the missing information carrier</h3><div class="checkpoint"><h4>Classify each artifact</h4><ol><li>“Fix the upload bug.” Is it an outcome prompt or an executable plan?</li><li>A file listing symptoms, reproduction, root cause, affected files, ordered tasks, and test commands: what makes it executable?</li><li>A document that describes the desired architecture but no validation: which boundary still requires a narrator?</li></ol><p class="fine">Pass if you classify by what a fresh agent can do from the artifact, not by file length or heading style.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: treating the plan as documentation</h3><p>The tell is that you generate a plan, open another session, then retype a long explanation before asking for implementation. Your second explanation is the real prompt; the plan is decorative. Fix it upstream: add the missing constraints, relevant files, order, and validation to the plan. Then open a genuinely fresh instance and pass only the executor plus the plan path.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Native plan mode complements durable specs</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>In-session planning surfaces can help draft and approve a direction, but the lesson’s <code>specs/</code> plans solve a different problem: they are durable, committable, team-visible inputs that can boot later agents. Use ephemeral planning to think; persist the approved executable plan when repeatability and handoff matter.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Close the section before revealing</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Plan as prompt retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Complete Dan’s ladder: “It’s a plan. It’s a ___ . It’s a ___.”</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A specification; a PRD. These are prompts scaled up for high impact.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What behavior proves a plan is the prompt?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A fresh agent can receive the plan as its instruction and carry the task without a human rebuilding hidden context in chat.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Teach it in ninety seconds</h4><p>Explain why “the plan is the prompt” is a testable engineering claim rather than a motivational slogan. Use the 749-line initial spec and the fresh-agent criterion as evidence.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your listener can distinguish a durable executable plan from documentation that still needs its author.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
-      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-anatomy">
-        <span class="section-number">04</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Five parts make the template legible</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">The course files are ordinary markdown. Their power comes from a disciplined anatomy: purpose, instructions, scope, output shape, and a parameterized work item.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The reusable prompt nobody can safely edit</h3><p>A team stores a large command prompt with background, examples, rules, requested change, and report format mixed together. It works for the author. A teammate cannot tell which lines define permanent process and which belong to one task. Scope grows accidentally; output headings drift; validation disappears during a cleanup.</p><p>Reusability requires more than saving a prompt. The stable contract must be visibly segmented so people and agents can reason about it.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the five-part spine</p><h3>Every part answers a different question</h3><div class="template-anatomy"><div><b>1 · Purpose</b><span>What artifact are we producing?</span></div><div><b>2 · Instructions</b><span>How should the agent reason and behave?</span></div><div><b>3 · Relevant Files</b><span>Where may research focus?</span></div><div><b>4 · Plan Format</b><span>What exact output contract must be filled?</span></div><div><b>5 · $ARGUMENTS</b><span>Which work instance is this run about?</span></div></div><p>Remove any one part and a different ambiguity appears: destination, behavior, scope, shape, or request.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — read the file, not the mystique</p><h3><code>chore.md</code> is compact enough to inspect end to end</h3><p>The file begins by ordering creation of a plan under <code>specs/*.md</code>. Its instructions demand thoroughness, repository research, placeholder replacement, deliberate reasoning, scope respect, and a README-first start. Relevant Files lists <code>README.md</code>, <code>app/**</code>, and <code>scripts/**</code>, then explicitly says to ignore everything else. The fenced Plan Format defines six output sections. The final H2 inserts the work request.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0660-chore-template-plan-format.png" alt="Editor showing the beginning of chore.md with purpose instructions relevant files and plan format" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:00</span>The first 34 lines expose four of the five parts. The final <code>## Chore</code> section holds <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>.</figcaption></figure></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — output contract</p><h3>Plan Format</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Plan Format:</strong> the exact markdown skeleton the planning agent fills. <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> is the command parameter receiving the user’s work request.</p></aside><p>A format is not cosmetic. Headings become an interface between planning and execution. They tell reviewers where to look for root cause or acceptance criteria, and they tell generic executors what information to expect without knowing the task.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — compare the shipped suite</p><h3>Shared skeleton, specialized evidence</h3><table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Physical lines</th><th>Special planning load</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>chore.md</code></td><td>53</td><td>Description, files, ordered tasks, validation, notes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bug.md</code></td><td>69</td><td>Adds problem/solution, reproduction, root cause, minimal surgical fix.</td></tr><tr><td><code>feature.md</code></td><td>91</td><td>Adds user story, three phases, testing strategy, edge cases, acceptance criteria.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>All three seed validation with <code>cd app/server &amp;&amp; uv run pytest</code>. That line is a regression floor, not a complete proof for every task. A generated plan should add task-specific checks rather than delete the baseline.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — anatomy audit</p><h3>Mark five parts and three specializations</h3><ol><li>Open <code>chore.md</code>, <code>bug.md</code>, and <code>feature.md</code> side by side.</li><li>Mark the same five parts in each.</li><li>Underline the minimal/surgical constraints in <code>bug.md</code>.</li><li>Circle the feature template’s three implementation phases.</li><li>Find every seeded pytest command and every final <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> slot.</li></ol><p>Do not edit. This is interface literacy: predict the output shape before firing a command.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: porting the prose but losing the guardrails</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> someone copies the headings into a new repo but removes “Ignore all other files,” the README-first research step, or the validation seed. The template looks familiar while its behavioral boundary is gone.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> port by responsibility. For each of the five parts, state what failure it prevents. Replace repository paths and commands deliberately; preserve the existence of scope and validation even when their contents change.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Metadata can harden the same interface</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Frontmatter can advertise description and argument hints, select a model, or constrain tools. Inline context features can inject file or command output. Treat these as ways to make purpose, input, and scope more machine-visible. They do not excuse a weak Plan Format or missing validation contract.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Publish syntax only after version-specific verification; this chapter’s course files remain the source of truth.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Template anatomy retrieval questions"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the five parts in order.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Purpose, Instructions, Relevant Files, Plan Format, and the final work-class section containing <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What two bug sections most directly prevent solution-first guessing?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Steps to Reproduce and Root Cause Analysis.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is the pytest line a seed rather than the entire strategy?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It protects the existing regression floor, while each generated plan must add evidence specific to its work.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach the template as an interface.</strong> For each of the five parts, explain the ambiguity it removes. Then show how bug and feature specialize that shared interface.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your listener could review a new template for missing responsibilities without copying TAC-3 verbatim.</p></div></div>
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="template-engineering">
+        <span class="section-number">02</span><h2 class="section-title">Template your engineering, not your code</h2><p class="section-lead">A code template repeats an artifact shape. An engineering template repeats a problem-solving shape: how to research, structure, validate, and report a class of work.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Planning from a blank page does not scale</h3><p>Hand-writing every plan feels responsible, but the cost repeats. You remember to add reproduction steps to one bug and forget them in the next. A feature plan contains acceptance criteria because the author was careful; a chore plan omits test commands because the author was rushed. The variability is not in the problem alone. It is in your planning process. Templates amortize that judgment.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — class in, plan out</p><h3>Three planning systems, one destination</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><div class="template-anatomy"><article><b>CHORE</b><p>Known maintenance outcome.</p></article><article><b>BUG</b><p>Symptoms → reproduction → root cause.</p></article><article><b>FEATURE</b><p>User value → phased design → acceptance.</p></article><article><b>OUTPUT</b><p>A reviewable plan in <code>specs/</code>.</p></article><article><b>EXECUTOR</b><p>The same <code>/implement</code> HOP.</p></article></div></div><figcaption>Specialize upstream planning; keep downstream execution generic.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — tactic three is located in the course arc</p><h3>The Pareto trio becomes a system</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0200-tactic-03-template-your-engineering.png" alt="Course slide for Tactic 03 Template Your Engineering with daily actions and eight-tactic indicator" width="1920" height="1080"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">3:20</span>Tactic 03 follows Stop Coding and Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective. The daily actions move from one-off prompting to meta-prompts, specialized templates, HOPs, refinement, and documented domain patterns.</figcaption></figure><p class="wide-intro">The slide’s body contains a small typo, so the clean lesson wording is the safer definition: encode problem solving into reusable units that you, your team, and your agents can see, use, and improve. That final verb prevents templates from becoming frozen bureaucracy. Outcomes are feedback for the next version.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term + plain English</p><h3>Template Your Engineering</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Tactic #3</p><p>Encode how you solve <em>classes</em> of work into reusable natural-language systems. The immediate product is a plan; the compounding product is an improvable record of how your engineering works.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the tactic on disk</p><h3>Four files are the entire mechanism</h3><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/commands/chore.md</code><p>53 lines: maintenance planning with description, files, ordered tasks, validation, and notes.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/commands/bug.md</code><p>69 lines: adds symptoms, expected/actual behavior, reproduction, root cause, and minimal-fix discipline.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/commands/feature.md</code><p>91 lines: adds user story, phased implementation, testing strategy, and acceptance criteria.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>.claude/commands/implement.md</code><p>12 displayed lines: executes any plan and reports a summary plus <code>git diff --stat</code>.</p></div></div><p class="wide-intro">There is no application code in these four files. Their power comes from putting team judgment into the agent’s instruction surface. The separate <code>/install</code> command reinforces the pattern by asking an agent to run reusable setup work, including <code>scripts/copy_dot_env.sh</code>.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — map work to process</p><h3>Choose a template by uncertainty</h3><div class="checkpoint"><ol><li>“Rename a button label everywhere it appears.” Which planner begins with the right shape?</li><li>“Uploaded identifiers can reach raw SQL.” Which missing sections would make a chore plan unsafe?</li><li>“Add query history with a collapsible panel.” Why do a user story and acceptance criteria matter?</li></ol><p class="fine">Suggested classification: chore; bug because reproduction/root cause are essential; feature because value and behavioral acceptance must be designed.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: one universal planning template</h3><p>Uniformity feels elegant but erases the questions that define the work. A bug needs a causal explanation and a before/after reproduction. A feature needs user value, integration phases, and acceptance boundaries. A chore benefits from a smaller surface. Fix the abstraction at the class boundary: share the common skeleton while specializing the plan format.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Templates became a packaging primitive</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Repo-local command templates remain useful for generating plans. Agent Skills add discoverability, frontmatter, on-demand loading, scripts, and team-scale packaging. The lesson’s design transfers directly: encode a repeatable class of work, define its context and checks, then improve it from outcomes.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Publication note: reverify current tool details and video metadata before release.</p></aside><div class="video-cards"><a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU"><span class="video-card__type">Current-practice bridge</span><strong>RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL</strong><p>Maps the lesson’s reusable template idea to the later Agent Skill packaging model.</p><span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span></a></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Template engineering retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does a TAC-3 planning template output?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A plan in <code>specs/</code>, not code.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">For which three audiences must encoded engineering remain visible and improvable?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>You, your team, and your agents.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Defend three templates</h4><p>Explain to a skeptical teammate why chore, bug, and feature planners should share a skeleton but not a plan format. Give one section whose absence would damage each work class.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the defense is about engineering risk and evidence, not personal formatting preference.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
-      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-plan">
-        <span class="section-number">01</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">The plan is the prompt</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A plan, specification, or PRD is not separate from prompting. It is prompting scaled to carry high-impact work across files, phases, and checks.</p>
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="meta-prompt">
+        <span class="section-number">03</span><h2 class="section-title">A meta prompt turns one sentence into a plan</h2><p class="section-lead">The human supplies the outcome. The template supplies the research procedure, boundaries, structure, and verification floor. The agent fills the gap.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Expecting the one-liner to contain the plan</h3><p>A high-level prompt is intentionally void of detail. If you force every file path, edge case, and test command into the invocation, you are hand-writing the plan inside a shell line. If you provide only the outcome with no planning template, you get shallow improvisation. The meta prompt resolves the tension: a small input enters a large, reusable planning procedure.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the expansion boundary</p><h3>Human intent plus encoded process</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><div class="phase-flow"><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">$ARGUMENTS</span><h4>One outcome sentence</h4><p>“Replace all server print statements with proper Python logging…”</p></article><div class="phase-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">+</div><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">CHORE.MD</span><h4>Research and plan contract</h4><p>Read README, inspect bounded files, fill exact sections, preserve behavior, seed validation.</p></article></div><p class="diagram-result"><strong>↓ agent research ↓</strong><br><code>specs/replace-print-with-logging.md</code></p></div><figcaption>The template holds repeated process; the invocation holds what is unique this time.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — one sentence goes in</p><h3>The agent receives a small target</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0470-chore-prompt-fired.png" alt="Course frame showing the chore prompt being typed below the install command file" width="1920" height="1080"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">7:50</span>The screen prompt names logging, levels, preserved output, and standard out. It does not inventory files or dictate every edit.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0585-generated-chore-plan.png" alt="Course frame showing the generated 64-line logging plan in specs and the one-sentence source prompt" width="1920" height="1080"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">9:45</span>The planning agent writes a 64-line spec with a per-line inventory. Ignore the prose summary’s off-by-one count; the verified repository total is 18 prints in <code>server.py</code> plus one in <code>main.py</code>.</figcaption></figure></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term + plain English</p><h3>Meta prompt</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Definition</p><p>A meta prompt is <strong>a prompt that builds a prompt</strong>. In TAC-3, <code>/chore</code>, <code>/bug</code>, and <code>/feature</code> are agentic prompts whose output is a plan another agent will execute.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — reconcile the evidence</p><h3>Grade the inventory, not the confident summary</h3><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Observed demo output</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>The on-camera agent says “20 across two files.” The generated line inventory visible in the frame does not support that prose total.</p></div></article><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>On-disk course truth</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><code>app/server/server.py</code> contains 18 <code>print(</code> lines; <code>app/server/main.py</code> contains one. Total: 19.</p></div></article></div><p class="wide-intro">This discrepancy is not a footnote to hide. It teaches how to operate generated plans: inspect enumerated evidence, cross-check the repository, and correct the plan before firing the implementer. “Plan is prompt” increases the importance of review because an error in the staging representation will be expanded into code.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — inspect the handoff</p><h3>Predict the missing sections</h3><div class="checkpoint"><p>Before opening <code>chore.md</code>, write the sections a logging plan must contain. Then compare your list with the actual format: Chore, Chore Description, Relevant Files, Step by Step Tasks, Validation Commands, Notes. Circle which sections come from the template rather than the one-sentence input.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you recognize that nearly all structural quality is encoded in the template.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: expecting <code>/chore</code> to edit code</h3><p>The command completes when the plan exists. That is phase one. Telling the same session “now implement it” collapses the learning architecture and carries hidden context forward. Fix: review the generated spec, commit or otherwise preserve it, open a fresh instance, and invoke <code>/implement specs/&lt;plan&gt;.md</code>.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Richer command surfaces do not change the composition</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Command files may now declare frontmatter, argument hints, models, allowed tools, positional arguments, file references, and injected command output. Those features can reduce discovery work, but the stable design remains: keep unique intent in the invocation and reusable process in the template.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Meta prompt retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define meta prompt without using the word template.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A prompt that builds another prompt; here it researches the repository and writes an executable plan.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the input and output artifacts in the logging demo?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Input: a one-sentence chore string passed through <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>. Output: the observed 64-line <code>specs/replace-print-with-logging.md</code> plan.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Explain the leverage boundary</h4><p>Teach why a short invocation and a detailed output do not imply magic. Identify exactly where the missing detail comes from and which evidence should be checked before implementation.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you name template instructions, repository research, exact plan format, and plan review.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="template-anatomy">
+        <span class="section-number">04</span><h2 class="section-title">Five parts make a reusable planner</h2><p class="section-lead">The files look like ordinary Markdown. Their leverage comes from clean boundaries: purpose, instructions, context scope, output contract, and the request slot.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The copied template that researches the wrong repository</h3><p>You copy <code>chore.md</code> into another project but leave <code>README.md</code>, <code>app/**</code>, <code>scripts/**</code>, and the Python test command untouched. The agent follows the template faithfully and produces a vague or irrelevant plan. The failure is not “AI does not understand my codebase.” You encoded the wrong search boundary and validation floor.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the five-part contract</p><h3>Each part answers one planning question</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><div class="template-anatomy"><article><b>1 · Purpose</b><p>What artifact must be created?</p></article><article><b>2 · Instructions</b><p>How should research and reasoning proceed?</p></article><article><b>3 · Relevant Files</b><p>Where may the agent look?</p></article><article><b>4 · Plan Format</b><p>What exact output must be filled?</p></article><article><b>5 · $ARGUMENTS</b><p>What is unique about this run?</p></article></div></div><figcaption>Reuse is possible because stable process and variable request occupy different slots.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the ordinary file is opened</p><h3>The template is legible enough to audit</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0660-chore-template-plan-format.png" alt="Course frame showing the first 34 lines of chore.md including purpose, instructions, relevant files, and plan format" width="1920" height="1080"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:00</span>The screen exposes the mechanism: purpose, eight instruction bullets including THINK HARD, a bounded relevant-files list, and the beginning of the exact plan format.</figcaption></figure><p class="wide-intro">The accessibility is the point. A teammate can review the planner the same way they review a runbook. They can see why the agent starts at <code>README.md</code>, why every placeholder must be replaced, why unrelated files are ignored, and which validation command every plan must retain.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term + plain English</p><h3>Plan format and argument parameter</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Two key seams</p><p>The <strong>Plan Format</strong> is the exact scaffold the planner fills. <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> is the variable slot receiving whatever follows the slash command. Stable form above; unique request below.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — compare specializations</p><h3>Same anatomy, different questions</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Template</th><th>Sections that reveal its engineering class</th><th>Risk being controlled</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>chore.md</code></td><td>Description, files, ordered tasks, validation, notes</td><td>Missing a maintenance detail or regression</td></tr><tr><td><code>bug.md</code></td><td>Symptoms, expected/actual, problem/solution, reproduction, root cause</td><td>Fixing the symptom or making a broad speculative change</td></tr><tr><td><code>feature.md</code></td><td>User story, phased implementation, testing strategy, acceptance criteria</td><td>Building code without user value, integration order, or a finish line</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="wide-intro">The bug template explicitly asks for surgical, minimal changes and says to keep the solution simple. The feature template asks the planner to research existing patterns and design for extensibility. These are not decorations; they are compressed review feedback placed before code exists.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — template audit</p><h3>Trace every instruction to its consequence</h3><div class="checkpoint"><ol><li>Open the three planning files in <code>tac/tac-3/.claude/commands/</code>.</li><li>Mark the five common parts.</li><li>For each unique plan section, write the failure it prevents.</li><li>Find the seeded <code>cd app/server &amp;&amp; uv run pytest</code> command and explain why removing it weakens every generated plan.</li></ol><p class="fine">Pass if your annotations connect form to risk rather than merely counting headings.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: placeholders survive into the plan</h3><p>A generated plan containing <code>&lt;describe the root cause&gt;</code> has not met the output contract. So has a ported template whose Relevant Files point at nonexistent directories. Fix the template, not the downstream prose: keep the instruction to replace every placeholder, make the search scope honest, and seed validation that actually runs in this repository.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Dynamic context can sharpen, not replace, file boundaries</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Modern command metadata and dynamic context can inject file lists or pin tools. Use them to make the five-part contract more explicit. Do not turn the planner into an unbounded context vacuum; “Relevant Files” is an engineering decision about where truth and risk live.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Template anatomy retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the five common template parts in order.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Purpose line; Instructions; Relevant Files; Plan Format; final work-class section containing <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name two sections bug planning adds beyond the chore shape.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Steps to Reproduce and Root Cause Analysis; Problem and Solution Statements are also valid.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Draw the planner from memory</h4><p>On a blank page, draw the five-part template. Then add the minimum unique sections for a bug and explain the causal role of each.</p><p class="fine">Pass if another engineer could recreate a safe bug planner from your drawing.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="hop">
+        <span class="section-number">05</span><h2 class="section-title">A Higher Order Prompt executes any plan</h2><p class="section-lead">Planning specialization creates many plan shapes. Execution stays composable because one tiny prompt accepts those plans as input.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The executor that knows too much</h3><p>You add logging-specific instructions to <code>implement.md</code>, then SQL-safety instructions, then UI conventions. Soon the “universal” executor is a second planning document with conflicting branches. Every new work class changes it. The abstraction is backwards: task detail belongs in the plan; the executor should provide the stable act of reading, implementing, and reporting.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — prompts passed into prompts</p><h3>Composition mirrors higher-order functions</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><div class="phase-flow"><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">INPUT PROMPT</span><h4><code>specs/&lt;plan&gt;.md</code></h4><p>Contains task-specific research, files, steps, validation, and notes.</p></article><div class="phase-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">HIGHER ORDER PROMPT</span><h4><code>/implement $ARGUMENTS</code></h4><p>Reads the plan, reasons, implements, summarizes, and reports the diff statistic.</p></article></div></div><figcaption>The plan supplies behavior-specific detail; the HOP supplies the reusable execution frame.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — definition, file, and effect share one frame</p><h3>Twelve displayed lines carry the build phase</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0815-higher-order-prompt-slide.png" alt="Course frame defining Higher Order Prompt with implement.md and a live logging diff behind it" width="1920" height="1080"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:35</span>The clean on-screen definition compares prompts accepting prompts to functions accepting functions. Behind it, <code>implement.md</code> receives <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> under <code>## Plan</code> while the logging diff flows.</figcaption></figure><p class="wide-intro">The file displays as twelve lines in the editor; <code>wc -l</code> reports eleven because the final line lacks a trailing newline. The operational fact is its smallness. It has a purpose, one instruction, a Plan slot, and a Report contract. Power arrives through the plan.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term + plain English</p><h3>Higher Order Prompt, “AKA a HOP”</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Definition</p><p>A prompt that accepts other prompts as input parameters, enabling composable agentic workflows. In this lesson, <code>/implement</code> accepts a plan file path.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the complete executor</p><h3>Four sections, one universal job</h3><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>Purpose</code><p>Follow the Instructions to implement the Plan, then Report completed work.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>## Instructions</code><p>Read the plan, think hard about it, and implement it.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>## Plan</code><p><code>$ARGUMENTS</code> receives the task-specific plan path.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>## Report</code><p>Concise bullets plus files and total lines changed from <code>git diff --stat</code>.</p></div></div><p class="wide-intro"><code>/implement specs/replace-print-with-logging.md</code> runs the logging plan. The same executor later runs the SQL-injection plan. This is the concrete payoff of composition: specialized plan producers converge on a stable plan-consuming interface.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — boundary test</p><h3>Where should this instruction live?</h3><div class="checkpoint"><p>Classify each addition: “preserve exact log message shapes,” “run the plan’s validation commands,” “after implementation, report deviations from the plan,” “sanitize SQL identifiers.” Put task-specific items in the plan; stable cross-plan behavior belongs in an executor or HOP variant.</p><p class="fine">Pass if logging and SQL detail stay out of the universal executor, while implement-and-review becomes a legitimate reusable variant.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: bloating the HOP</h3><p>A larger executor is not automatically stronger. If it duplicates plan sections or names application files, it couples execution to one task and makes conflicting sources of truth. Fix by moving task detail into the spec. Keep only cross-plan behavior—implement, validate, review, report—in the HOP.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>The interface survives changing runtimes</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Whether execution happens through a slash command, skill, subagent, SDK call, or workflow node, the deep interface remains useful: accept one durable plan artifact, perform bounded work, return checkable evidence. Runtime packaging can change without rewriting the plan schema.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="HOP retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are <code>implement.md</code>’s four parts?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Purpose, Instructions, Plan with <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>, and Report.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is its smallness an architectural feature?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It stays generic across all plans; task-specific power flows into it through the plan file.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Use the function analogy precisely</h4><p>Explain meta prompt versus HOP using inputs and outputs. Use <code>/chore</code> and <code>/implement</code>, then say where validation responsibility lives.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you say: meta prompt produces a plan; HOP consumes a plan; the plan carries task validation and the executor runs the work/report contract.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="reasoning">
+        <span class="section-number">06</span><h2 class="section-title">Buy reasoning where leverage is highest</h2><p class="section-lead">The course-era phrase <code>THINK HARD</code> is period-specific. The durable move is deliberate: spend more reasoning compute while the plan is still cheap to change and expensive to get wrong.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Saving tokens before the irreversible decisions</h3><p>An agent races through planning, guesses the root cause, produces a plausible task list, and then spends a large implementation run correcting those guesses in code. The cheap phase was starved; the expensive phase absorbed the uncertainty. A second failure is the mirror image: writing “think hard” on every trivial command until the phrase becomes ritual rather than resource allocation.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — reason before expansion</p><h3>Planning is the compression point</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><div class="phase-flow"><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">PLAN</span><h4>Small artifact, large consequence</h4><p>Resolve scope, dependencies, root cause, order, tests, and completion evidence.</p></article><div class="phase-flow__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div><article class="phase-card"><span class="phase-card__tag">IMPLEMENT</span><h4>Large artifact, inherited decisions</h4><p>Expand the approved natural-language representation into files, commands, diffs, and reports.</p></article></div></div><figcaption>Reasoning invested before expansion can prevent many downstream edits; reasoning added after a weak plan often repairs avoidable damage.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the keyword appears in both phases</p><h3>The planner and executor explicitly request thought</h3><p><code>chore.md</code>, <code>bug.md</code>, and <code>feature.md</code> each ask the agent to use its reasoning model and “THINK HARD.” <code>implement.md</code> says to read the plan, think hard about it, and implement it. In the lesson’s Claude Code era, Dan describes “think” as an information-dense keyword that activates additional reasoning.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0660-chore-template-plan-format.png" alt="Course editor showing chore.md instructions including THINK HARD" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:00</span>The phrase sits beside concrete research and scope instructions. Its useful role is not motivational tone; it allocates reasoning to a planning-grade task.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s term + durable translation</p><h3>Information-dense keyword</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Course-era mechanism</p><p><strong>THINK HARD:</strong> a literal phrase Dan uses to activate more reasoning in the model available during the course. <strong>Durable principle:</strong> match reasoning budget to problem difficulty, especially before a plan is expanded into code.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the SQL bug</p><h3>Reasoning separates a scary label from the actual surfaces</h3><p>“SQL statements are not being escaped” is not yet a root cause. The shipped repository has a ten-keyword substring blocklist followed by raw <code>cursor.execute(sql_query)</code> in <code>core/sql_processor.py</code>. It also interpolates identifiers into SQL, including <code>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS {table_name}</code> in <code>server.py</code>. SQLite’s <code>execute()</code> rejects stacked statements, so a plan that assumes every classic multi-statement payload will work is shallow.</p><p>Planning-grade reasoning must distinguish value parameterization, identifier validation, query-shape restrictions, direct test surfaces, and UI/LLM nondeterminism. That analysis determines which code and tests belong in the fix before implementation begins.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — budget decision</p><h3>Choose where to spend the next thousand reasoning tokens</h3><div class="checkpoint"><p>Compare two tasks: correcting a typo in a README and designing a minimal SQL-safety fix across query values and identifiers. For each, list unknowns, blast radius, and proof. Assign a low, medium, or high planning budget and justify it.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the security task receives more investigation because uncertainty and consequences are greater—not because its prompt contains stronger adjectives.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: cargo-cult incantation</h3><p>Repeatedly writing “think hard” does not replace relevant context, a clear output contract, or tests. If the agent lacks the right files or the template asks the wrong questions, extra reasoning may produce a more articulate wrong plan. Fix the information surface first, then allocate reasoning in proportion to the decision.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Controls change; budget discipline survives</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Newer models and tools may expose first-class thinking controls or manage budgets more autonomously. Date the literal keyword to the course. Keep the architectural lesson: spend reasoning before high-impact choices, measure whether it improves the plan, and avoid paying planning costs on trivial work.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Reasoning budget retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What did THINK HARD mean in the course?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>An information-dense keyword intended to activate additional reasoning in course-era Claude Code.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What principle transfers when the literal mechanism changes?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Deliberately allocate more reasoning compute to difficult, high-impact planning work before decisions expand into code.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Separate mechanism from principle</h4><p>Teach why the words <code>THINK HARD</code> may age while the decision to buy reasoning at the planning compression point remains current.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your explanation avoids both magical thinking and blanket token minimization.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="fresh-agents">
+        <span class="section-number">07</span><h2 class="section-title">Fresh agents turn context into a testable interface</h2><p class="section-lead">A rolling conversation feels informed. A fresh instance is deliberately ignorant. That ignorance reveals whether your prompts, plans, and templates actually carry the work.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>“It worked yesterday in my session”</h3><p>The planner explored the repository, rejected two approaches, and generated a thin spec. The same session then implemented successfully because it remembered the discarded approaches and unstated constraints. A teammate opens the spec tomorrow and fails. The conversation was the hidden dependency; the artifact only appeared reusable.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — three reasons, four KPIs</p><h3>Isolation is both diagnosis and scale preparation</h3><div class="template-anatomy"><article><b>Focus</b><p>Give every available context token to the task at hand.</p></article><article><b>Assets</b><p>Force prompts and plans to have zero conversational dependencies.</p></article><article><b>Off-device</b><p>Prepare the same units to run where no person maintains the chat.</p></article><article><b>Known boot</b><p>Executor + plan define the starting perspective.</p></article><article><b>Improvement trail</b><p>Failures point back to inspectable files.</p></article></div><div class="kpi-grid"><div class="kpi"><b>↑</b><span>size</span></div><div class="kpi"><b>↓</b><span>attempts</span></div><div class="kpi"><b>↑</b><span>streak</span></div><div class="kpi"><b>→ 0</b><span>presence</span></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — eight terminals make the doctrine visible</p><h3>Dan repeatedly boots memoryless workers</h3><p>The lesson uses separate instances for installation, chore planning, chore implementation, bug planning, bug implementation, and headless feature planning. Dan asks why he keeps abandoning useful context and answers emphatically: the system must decouple performance from the conversational context window.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t1390-fresh-agents-terminals.jpg" alt="Course frame with implement.md, a plan-mirroring todo list, and eight terminal sessions stacked at right" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">23:10</span>The agent’s todo list mirrors the bug plan while the terminal rail records fresh instances. Isolation is an operating practice, not a conceptual aside.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s doctrine</p><h3>Fresh agents, every phase</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Three reasons</p><p>Free context for the current task; force isolated, reusable, improvable, zero-dependency assets; prepare for true off-device agentic coding. The goal is one-shot success with a known perspective at boot.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — isolate the logging handoff</p><h3>What the implementer may know</h3><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Allowed context</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><ul><li>The repository at its committed state</li><li><code>.claude/commands/implement.md</code></li><li>The reviewed logging plan path</li><li>Tool permissions and observable command results</li></ul></div></article><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Hidden dependencies to reject</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><ul><li>Why the planner omitted a file</li><li>Constraints mentioned only in chat</li><li>A remembered manual workaround</li><li>“You know what I meant” corrections</li></ul></div></article></div><p class="wide-intro">If the fresh implementer cannot preserve stdout, the response is not to resume the planning chat. Add the missing requirement to the plan or template, regenerate if needed, and rerun. The failure creates an improvement trail.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — dependency audit</p><h3>Make hidden memory visible</h3><p>Take a recent successful agent conversation. List every fact introduced after the original request. Mark which facts appear in a durable file. For every unrecorded fact needed to reproduce the result, choose its home: repository documentation, template, plan, test, or executor contract.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: confusing amnesia with inefficiency</h3><p>A fresh agent repeats some repository reading, which can look wasteful. But repeated deterministic priming is cheaper than an irreproducible success that depends on unknown chat state. Fix the repeated cost by improving concise repository docs and scoped research instructions—not by preserving an opaque conversation forever.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>The mechanics are easier; the doctrine is unchanged</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Clear/compact operations, isolated subagents, background tasks, and worktree-based parallelism can mechanize context boundaries. They do not make isolation automatic. Define one task, known inputs, bounded tools, and an output contract for each worker; otherwise parallel agents multiply hidden assumptions.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Fresh agent retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Recite the three reasons for fresh agents.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Focus context on one task; force zero-dependency reusable/improvable assets; prepare for off-device runs.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which KPI ultimately approaches zero?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Human presence. The associated direction is size up, attempts down, streak up, presence toward zero.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What should be knowable at implementation boot?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The agent’s perspective should derive from the repository, the generic executor, and the plan—not hidden planning chat.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Defend the empty context window</h4><p>Explain why a fresh agent is simultaneously a reliability test, an architecture constraint, and preparation for off-device execution. Tie it to all four KPIs.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the listener stops treating chat memory as a free dependency.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="validation">
+        <span class="section-number">08</span><h2 class="section-title">Plans must specify how they will be disproved</h2><p class="section-lead">Validation commands, focused tests, diff review, and manual reproduction turn the plan from a story about success into a loop that can detect failure.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>“Tests pass” while the behavior moved</h3><p>The logging agent replaces prints, runs the existing suite, and declares success. But no test asserts stdout message shape, and one call still prints. Or the SQL fix adds tests for keyword blocking while identifier interpolation remains. A green command proves only what it covers. Self-validation begins when the plan names the relevant observations, not when the agent emits a check mark.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — nested evidence</p><h3>One check cannot carry every claim</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Regression floor</strong><code>uv run pytest</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Task invariant</strong><code>rg 'print\('</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Behavior</strong><code>app output / direct test</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Change review</strong><code>git diff</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Report</strong><code>git diff --stat</code></div></div><p>The agent can execute this stack, but the reviewer still checks whether the stack matches the claims.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — precise diff, then bug plan</p><h3>The plan’s tasks become observable change</h3><p>The logging plan inventories exact call sites and maps success messages to informational logging and error paths to error logging. During implementation, the diff replaces those calls. Later the bug plan adds root-cause analysis and a proposed <code>sql_security.py</code>, forcing the security change to have an explicit mechanism rather than a generic “escape inputs” instruction.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0945-implement-report-git-diff.png" alt="Course frame showing a plan task list and red-to-green print-to-logger code diff" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">15:45</span>Plan tasks and diff align: the implementation is reviewable against a prior natural-language representation.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t1285-bug-plan-root-cause.png" alt="Course frame showing SQL injection bug plan root cause relevant files and a proposed sql_security module" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">21:25</span>The bug template forces root cause, relevant files, new files, and ordered tasks before implementation.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s leverage point</p><h3>Self-validating loops with tests</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Course language</p><p>Every Plan Format ends with <strong>Validation Commands</strong>; the final task runs them. The agent also reviews its own diff and can repair discrepancies before reporting.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — grade invariants, not magic totals</p><h3>Logging and SQL require different evidence</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Calibration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regression-free baseline</td><td><code>cd app/server &amp;&amp; uv run pytest</code></td><td>The shipped repo collects 34 tests. There are 37 <code>test_*</code> definitions, but three are fixtures.</td></tr><tr><td>No raw server prints</td><td><code>rg -n 'print\(' app/server/server.py app/server/main.py</code></td><td>Require zero hits after the “all server prints” chore.</td></tr><tr><td>Message semantics preserved</td><td>Direct behavior or app run shows the same <code>[SUCCESS]</code>/<code>[ERROR]</code> payload shapes on stdout.</td><td>Do not infer this from pytest unless a test asserts it.</td></tr><tr><td>SQL risk reduced</td><td>New focused cases exercise raw query and identifier surfaces; all baseline tests remain green.</td><td>Dan’s 18-new/52-total result is one run, not the grading constant.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — claim-to-check matrix</p><h3>Write the evidence before implementation</h3><p>For the logging chore, list four claims and one independent check per claim. For the SQL bug, include at least one direct unit or integration check that does not depend on an LLM turning a UI sentence into a specific SQL string. Mark which check the agent runs and which one you personally repeat.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: trusting or rejecting self-validation wholesale</h3><p>Blind trust treats the agent report as proof. Blind rejection makes the human rerun everything without improving the system. The repair is calibrated redundancy: embed deterministic checks in the plan, let the agent run them, inspect the diff and raw results, then independently reproduce the risk or key behavior. Feed missed checks back upstream.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Closed loops can be enforced outside the prompt</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>CI, hooks, sandboxes, test gates, and verifier agents can enforce the same validation contract. Prompted self-review remains useful, but evidence is stronger when the environment independently blocks or reports violations. Durable plans should name both agent-run checks and external gates.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Validation retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name two self-validation mechanisms shown in the lesson.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Plan-embedded Validation Commands and the agent’s review of its own diff followed by fixes.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does the human still do?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Inspect the plan/diff and independently repeat key behavior or reproduction plus the test command.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the real shipped baseline test count?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>34 collected tests. The 37 test-named functions include three pytest fixtures.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Explain why “tests passed” is incomplete</h4><p>Use the logging and SQL examples to show how claims, checks, coverage boundaries, diff review, and human reproduction fit together.</p><p class="fine">Pass if you grade invariants and evidence rather than repeating the video’s run-specific totals.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="headless">
+        <span class="section-number">09</span><h2 class="section-title">Headless planning reveals the machine interface</h2><p class="section-lead">The same feature template that works interactively can run through <code>claude -p</code>, stream structured events, and leave a plan on disk while nobody maintains a conversational session.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The automation that still needs a person watching</h3><p>A planning prompt is reusable, but someone must open the tool, paste it, wait, notice success, and tell the next system what happened. The prompt is encoded; the trigger and output interface are not. Off-device agentic coding requires the same unit to run from another program and emit machine-readable evidence.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — invisible agent, visible artifacts</p><h3>Standard input and output become composition seams</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Caller</strong><code>plain terminal / program</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Invocation</strong><code>claude -p "/feature …"</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Event stream</strong><code>stream-json → .jsonl</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Deliverable</strong><code>specs/query-history-panel.md</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Next node</strong><code>inspect / route / implement</code></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — a plan appears without an interactive agent</p><h3>Command, heartbeat, deliverable</h3><p>Dan runs the feature template in Claude Code’s programmatic form, selects stream JSON, bypasses permissions for this narrowly scoped demo, chooses an Opus model, requests verbose output, and redirects the stream into <code>feature_plan_raw_agent_logs.jsonl</code>. The file appends system, assistant, and tool-result events. The model visible in the captured JSONL is <code>claude-opus-4-20250514</code>.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t2205-claude-p-command.png" alt="Course frame showing the complete claude -p feature planning command and flags" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">36:45</span>The full headless command turns the existing <code>/feature</code> template into a callable planning phase.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t2270-jsonl-stream-live.png" alt="Course frame showing JSONL system assistant and tool events appending live" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">37:50</span>The JSONL is not decorative logging. It is the event interface another program can parse while the planner runs.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t2575-feature-spec-query-history.png" alt="Course frame showing the generated query history side panel feature plan" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">42:55</span>The planning-only run leaves a feature spec with Feature Description, User Story, and Problem Statement. Dan reviews it but does not implement it.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s bridge</p><h3>Programmatic form</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Course language</p><p><strong>Claude Code in its programmatic form:</strong> a prompt runs non-interactively through <code>claude -p</code>. <code>--output-format stream-json</code> exposes structured progress that can be chained, observed, and checked.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — read the historical command safely</p><h3>Separate reproducible shape from era-specific choices</h3><pre><code>claude -p "/feature \"Create a new query history side panel…\"" \
+  --output-format stream-json \
+  --dangerously-skip-permissions \
+  --model opus \
+  --verbose \
+  &gt; feature_plan_raw_agent_logs.jsonl</code></pre><p>The durable shape is prompt mode + template invocation + structured stream + captured artifact. The exact model alias drifts. The global permission bypass is not a recommendation; Dan scoped it to a low-risk planning run in a demo repository. Even there, the safer lab posture is a disposable standalone clone with no valuable secrets or changes.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — parse before you chain</p><h3>Prove the stream corresponds to the plan</h3><ol><li>Run a planning-only feature command from a plain terminal in the disposable TAC-3 clone.</li><li>Watch the JSONL append with <code>tail -f</code>.</li><li>Parse every nonblank line as JSON.</li><li>Confirm the stream has initialization plus assistant/tool activity.</li><li>Confirm a new <code>specs/*.md</code> file has a user story, three implementation phases, testing strategy, and acceptance criteria.</li><li>Do not implement in this checkpoint.</li></ol></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: “planning-only” becomes a blanket safety exemption</h3><p>A planning prompt can still read files, execute tools, or write artifacts. <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code> removes a control; intent does not make that control irrelevant. Fix by narrowing environment, credentials, filesystem scope, tools, and permissions. Prefer allowlists, permission modes, hooks, or sandboxing when available. Preserve the course command as historical evidence, not a default production posture.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Structured streams remain; production embedding gets stronger boundaries</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Tool allowlists, permission modes, hooks, sandboxed execution, and agent SDKs provide more deliberate integration paths. The stream concept remains central: callers need structured lifecycle and tool events, not a terminal transcript scraped as prose.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Optional supplement from the extract: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6fCqj4xFCZI">programmable/headless current-practice video</a>. Verify current flags against official documentation before production use.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Headless retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which template ran headlessly, and what plan appeared?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>/feature</code>; the observed result was <code>specs/query-history-panel.md</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the four displayed flags after the prompt.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>--output-format stream-json</code>, <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code>, <code>--model opus</code>, and <code>--verbose</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why is JSONL more than “logs”?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is a line-delimited machine-readable event interface that another process can observe, parse, route, and validate.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Explain the bridge to lesson four</h4><p>Walk from the same repo-local <code>/feature</code> template to a noninteractive caller, event stream, and durable plan. Then explain why permission bypass and programmatic execution are separate design decisions.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the listener can preserve composition while tightening safety.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="specialize">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span><h2 class="section-title">Specialize upstream, share the system, start small</h2><p class="section-lead">Generic agent tools know how to act. Your templates teach them how this codebase and team decide. That encoded specificity is where repeatable engineering becomes differentiated engineering.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The powerful agent set loose without your process</h3><p>A new model arrives with better coding benchmarks. The team stops maintaining its templates and asks the model to “figure it out.” It produces impressive local changes but misses the database migration ritual, the team’s UI boundaries, or the exact acceptance evidence. Capability improved; organization-specific knowledge vanished from the interface.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the self-improving loop</p><h3>Failures become edits to shared assets</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Run</strong><code>template + request</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Observe</strong><code>plan / build / evidence</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Locate</strong><code>template? plan? HOP?</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Improve</strong><code>committed asset</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Share</strong><code>team’s next run</code></div></div><p>A conversational correction helps once. A template correction changes every future run of that class.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — the comprehensive plan is the differentiation</p><h3>Your process survives the invisible agent</h3><p>The headless feature planner produces a substantial query-history plan because <code>feature.md</code> encodes user story, phased implementation, testing strategy, and acceptance criteria. The tool did not arrive knowing this repository’s desired planning shape. The codebase supplied it.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t2575-feature-spec-query-history.png" alt="Generated query history plan with feature description and user story" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">42:55</span>The output makes specialization tangible: a codebase-local planning system shapes a model’s general capability into a team-reviewable feature prompt.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s standing order</p><h3>Build the system that builds the system</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Lesson conclusion</p><p>“The more custom your templates are, the more unique your engineering gets.” Agents running loose without structure are a plan for failure. Customization belongs in encoded process, not endless supervision.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — specialization ladder</p><h3>Move from generic class to domain-specific planner</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Starting template</th><th>Useful specialization</th><th>What changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>feature.md</code></td><td>Front-end feature planner</td><td>Relevant client paths, UI pattern research, browser checks, accessibility acceptance.</td></tr><tr><td><code>chore.md</code></td><td>Database migration chore</td><td>Migration files, rollback discipline, schema checks, data-safety validation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>bug.md</code></td><td>Research-notebook bug resolver</td><td>Notebook state, reproduction fixtures, deterministic cells, artifact comparison.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are lesson examples of the specialization direction, not claims about files shipped in TAC-3. The safe port keeps the five responsibilities and replaces paths, constraints, and checks with truths from the target repository.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — smallest useful customization</p><h3>Improve one planning question</h3><p>Inside the course repo, duplicate a template only for a stretch exercise. Restrict it to a real subsystem, change Relevant Files honestly, and add one validation requirement the generic version lacks. Fire the generic and specialized planners on the same class-shaped request. Diff the plans; identify which differences came from encoded process rather than model luck.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: waiting for the perfect template</h3><p>The belief “my codebase is too big” turns template design into an all-or-nothing migration. Another trap is polishing a mega-template before it has produced one plan. Fix with Dan’s explicit call to action: run one small chore now. Let the first plan expose one missing rule. Improve that rule. Compounding begins with a cheap, bounded loop.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Packaging scales, ownership remains local</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Skills, shared command packages, policy hooks, and agent layers can distribute encoded expertise more broadly than copied markdown. The durable ownership question is unchanged: who reviews the process, which repository facts ground it, how are failures fed back, and can a teammate tell which version produced an outcome?</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Specialization retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why do custom templates matter even as models improve?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>General tools do not inherently know the codebase’s architecture, team workflow, domain risks, and evidence standards. Templates encode that local judgment.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Where should a recurring plan failure be repaired?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>At the reusable upstream layer that caused it—template for class-wide planning gaps, plan for one-task facts, HOP for cross-plan execution/reporting gaps.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the lesson’s call to action?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Do not wait; run one small <code>/chore</code> now and begin the improvement loop.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><h4>Teach the compounding mechanism</h4><p>Explain how one failed run becomes shared engineering leverage. Name the artifact you would edit, the evidence that justified the edit, and how a fresh-agent rerun tests the improvement.</p><p class="fine">Pass if the feedback loop ends in a versioned asset rather than a remembered conversational correction.</p></div></div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="lab">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span><h2 class="section-title">Graded lab · plan, build, verify, disappear</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use only the TAC-3 course repository. You will reproduce the lesson’s two-phase chore and bug workflows, then prove the feature planner works without an interactive session. Grade from files and behavior, not from a persuasive agent report.</p>
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning"><p class="callout__label">Safety and scope contract</p><p>All work remains inside the cloned <code>tac-3</code> repository. Type zero application code by hand. Preserve the repository deny rules for force-push forms and <code>rm -rf</code>. The demo’s permission-bypass flag is limited to the required planning-only headless run.</p></aside>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3.0 · Establish the starting state</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>10–15 min</li><li>Course repo only</li><li>Evidence: settings + working app</li></ul>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Clone the course repository and enter it: <code>git clone https://github.com/disler/tac-3.git &amp;&amp; cd tac-3</code>.</li><li>Read <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. State the 12 allowed entries and the three denied command patterns in your own words. Do not widen the permissions.</li><li>Choose one API-key path. For the filmed path, keep TAC-2 as a sibling with <code>app/server/.env</code> populated so <code>scripts/copy_dot_env.sh</code> can copy it. Otherwise copy <code>app/server/.env.sample</code> to <code>app/server/.env</code> and add an OpenAI or Anthropic key; the sibling-copy step may report its expected error.</li><li>Open Claude Code and run <code>/install</code>. Confirm its report says priming ran, frontend and backend dependencies were installed, and an environment path was handled.</li><li>In a new terminal run <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh</code>. Confirm backend port 8000 and frontend port 5173.</li><li>Use the sample <code>users.json</code> data and ask: <strong>Show users with age between 20 and 40</strong>. Record the successful baseline and note the raw server <code>print</code> output.</li></ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 0 pass</h4><ul><li>□ You can explain what the agent may and may not do from the settings file.</li><li>□ Both services are reachable.</li><li>□ The baseline query works before any lesson change.</li><li>□ You know which stdout lines the logging chore will target.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-chore-plan"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3.1A · Generate the logging plan</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Fresh planning agent</li><li>Meta prompt</li><li>Artifact: one spec</li></ul>
+          <p>Open a fresh Claude Code instance and run:</p>
+          <div class="terminal"><div class="terminal__bar">fresh agent A · planning only</div><pre><code>/chore "Replace all server print statements with proper python logging. Use proper log levels. Preserve the exact same log output. Make sure all output is written to standard out"</code></pre></div>
+          <p>While it works, locate all five template parts in <code>.claude/commands/chore.md</code>. When the plan lands, review it before implementation. Confirm the six required sections: Chore, Chore Description, Relevant Files, Step by Step Tasks, Validation Commands, Notes.</p>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 1A pass</h4><ul><li>□ A new plan exists under <code>specs/</code>.</li><li>□ Its inventory includes <code>app/server/server.py</code> and <code>app/server/main.py</code>.</li><li>□ You reconcile the disk truth as 18 + 1 = 19 print calls rather than copying the video agent’s off-by-one prose.</li><li>□ The plan preserves the <code>[SUCCESS]</code>/<code>[ERROR]</code> message shapes and stdout behavior.</li><li>□ The plan contains the seeded pytest command and a final validation step.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
 
-        <div class="move move--hook">
-          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
-          <h3>The detailed request that still leaves every hard decision downstream</h3>
-          <p>You ask an agent to “fix logging throughout the server, preserve behavior, use appropriate levels, and run tests.” The sentence sounds responsible. Yet the implementer must still discover every print, infer the intended message shapes, decide where logging is configured, choose an order, know which files are in scope, and determine which checks establish equivalence. The request names the destination but leaves the route implicit.</p>
-          <p>When the agent misses a call site, changes output shape, or stops after tests, the failure looks like weak coding. Often it is weak planning. The implementation prompt did not contain enough resolved engineering judgment to make success reproducible.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--picture">
-          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — information density</p>
-          <h3>One destination, two payloads</h3>
-          <div class="comparison">
-            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Request</h3><p>Names the desired change.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><code>Replace server prints with logging.</code><p>Useful trigger; insufficient implementation contract.</p></div></article>
-            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Plan</h3><p>Resolves the work before execution.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><code>inventory → files → ordered tasks → validation → notes</code><p>A prompt with enough structure to be consumed independently.</p></div></article>
-          </div>
-          <p>The difference is not length alone. A long paragraph may still hide sequencing and proof. A strong plan externalizes the decisions the next agent must otherwise rediscover.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--story">
-          <p class="move-label">3 · Story — the repository already proves it</p>
-          <h3>A 749-line plan precedes this lesson</h3>
-          <p>The TAC-3 repository contains <code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code>, a 749-line plan for the application carried forward from TAC-2. The file sits beside the finished client and server. It is the chapter’s quiet precondition: the application can be understood as the execution of a large, durable prompt.</p>
-          <p>Dan’s opening claim therefore has a concrete referent. Plans are “prompts scaled up for high impact.” A plan can specify work beyond one response, preserve intent after a chat ends, and give a fresh executor the same starting artifact a teammate would receive.</p>
-          <figure class="frame-card">
-            <img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0200-tactic-03-template-your-engineering.png" alt="Course slide for Tactic 03 Template Your Engineering with daily actions" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
-            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">3:20</span>Tactic 03 locates planning inside a repeatable practice: create meta prompts, specialize templates, build HOPs, refine what fails, and document how the system works.</figcaption>
-          </figure>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--name">
-          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan’s equation</p>
-          <h3>Great Planning === Great Prompting</h3>
-          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Plan / spec / PRD:</strong> a prompt scaled up for high-impact work. The plan is the prompt; therefore great planning is great prompting.</p></aside>
-          <p>In plain English, planning is the act of assembling the context and decisions that make an execution request trustworthy. A plan does not need to predict every implementation token. It must remove avoidable ambiguity about purpose, relevant surfaces, ordering, constraints, proof, and completion.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--example">
-          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — upgrade a request</p>
-          <h3>From “replace prints” to an executable contract</h3>
-          <p>Begin with the one-line outcome. Research the repository. The shipped baseline contains 18 <code>print(</code> calls in <code>app/server/server.py</code> and one in <code>app/server/main.py</code>. The server messages already carry <code>[SUCCESS]</code> and <code>[ERROR]</code> shapes. Tests live under <code>app/server/tests/</code>; pytest collects 34 actual tests from 37 <code>test_*</code> function definitions because three of those functions are fixtures.</p>
-          <p>A plan can now say which files change, which call sites map to <code>logger.info</code> or <code>logger.error</code>, where configuration belongs, how stdout behavior remains observable, and which commands establish zero regressions. That is qualitatively different from adding adjectives like “thoroughly” to the original sentence.</p>
-          <div class="workflow">
-            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Inventory</strong><code>19 shipped prints</code></div>
-            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Preserve</strong><code>[SUCCESS]/[ERROR]</code></div>
-            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Implement</strong><code>levels + stdout</code></div>
-            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Prove</strong><code>pytest + grep + app</code></div>
-          </div>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--try">
-          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — five-minute conversion</p>
-          <h3>Turn one vague change into plan headings</h3>
-          <ol><li>Choose one small change visible in <code>tac/tac-3</code>.</li><li>Write the outcome in one sentence.</li><li>Without implementing, add headings for relevant files, ordered tasks, validation commands, and notes.</li><li>Under each heading, write one uncertainty that research must resolve.</li><li>Circle every claim another agent could verify without your chat history.</li></ol>
-          <p>You pass this drill when the plan exposes missing knowledge instead of disguising it with confident prose.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--fail">
-          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
-          <h3>Failure: treating a checklist as a plan</h3>
-          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the document says “update backend, add tests, run tests.” It names categories but contains no file reasoning, dependencies, observable behavior, or completion evidence.</p>
-          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> require each task to answer three questions: where does the change land, what existing behavior constrains it, and what evidence makes the result falsifiable? If the next agent must rediscover the answer, the plan is still an outline.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--current">
-          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
-          <h3>Ephemeral planning and durable specs solve different problems</h3>
-          <aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agent tools may offer an in-session plan mode. That can help a human inspect and approve a proposed route. It does not replace a committed <code>specs/</code> artifact when the plan must survive the session, be reviewed by a team, feed another agent, and improve over time. Use an interactive planning mode to draft; persist the result when durability and composition matter.</p><p class="current-practice__source">The transferable principle is artifact durability, not loyalty to one planning interface.</p></aside>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--check">
-          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
-          <h3>Answer before opening</h3>
-          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Plan is prompt retrieval questions">
-            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Complete both chapter equations.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The plan is the prompt. Great planning is great prompting.</p></details></div>
-            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is a long request not automatically a strong plan?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Length can still hide scope, sequence, constraints, relevant files, and proof. A plan externalizes resolved engineering decisions.</p></details></div>
-            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What existing file proves the pattern before the lesson begins?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code>, the 749-line plan associated with the application carried from TAC-2.</p></details></div>
-          </section>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--teach">
-          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
-          <div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Teach the equation without using the word “detail.”</strong> Explain how a plan changes the independence, durability, and verifiability of an agent’s implementation prompt. Use the logging chore as evidence.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your listener can explain why the plan remains useful after the original conversation disappears.</p></div>
-        </div>
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-chore-build"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3.1B · Execute with the HOP</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Fresh build agent</li><li>No planning chat</li><li>Evidence: tests + grep + UI + report</li></ul>
+          <p>Preserve the reviewed plan as your phase boundary. Open a new instance and run <code>/implement specs/&lt;your-chore-plan&gt;.md</code>. Do not re-explain the chore. Watch whether the todo list mirrors the plan’s ordered tasks.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Run <code>cd app/server &amp;&amp; uv run pytest</code>; require zero regressions against the 37 pre-existing tests.</li><li>Search application Python files with <code>grep -rn "print(" app/server --include="*.py"</code>. Grade the target application files, not test fixtures. <code>server.py</code> should have no remaining target print calls; <code>main.py</code> may be converted as well.</li><li>Restart through <code>scripts/start.sh</code>, then ask <strong>get users id name sorted by signup date descending</strong>.</li><li>Confirm the same success/error message shapes flow through logging to stdout.</li><li>Read the agent’s report. It must contain concise bullets plus <code>git diff --stat</code>.</li></ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 1B pass</h4><ul><li>□ Planning and building happened in different agent instances.</li><li>□ Tests are green.</li><li>□ The grep and diff agree with the plan inventory.</li><li>□ The application still performs the baseline class of query.</li><li>□ Report claims match your independent observations.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-bug-plan"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3.2A · Generate a causal bug plan</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Fresh planning agent</li><li>Bug template</li><li>Evidence: reproduction + root cause</li></ul>
+          <p>After recording the chore checkpoint in Git, open a fresh instance and run:</p>
+          <div class="terminal"><div class="terminal__bar">fresh agent C · planning only</div><pre><code>/bug "Resolve major issue: our SQL statements are not being escaped — user input flows into SQL"</code></pre></div>
+          <p>Do not accept generic “sanitize input” language. Inspect the actual course files. The plan should discover the 10-keyword substring blocklist and raw <code>cursor.execute(sql_query)</code> in <code>core/sql_processor.py</code>, identifier interpolation across schema-related code, and the delete-table f-string at <code>server.py:243</code>.</p>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 2A pass</h4><ul><li>□ Steps to Reproduce is non-empty and executable.</li><li>□ Root Cause Analysis distinguishes SQL values from identifiers.</li><li>□ Relevant Files names the real surfaces in <code>sql_processor.py</code>, <code>file_processor.py</code>, <code>insights.py</code>, and <code>server.py</code> where appropriate.</li><li>□ The plan proposes minimal, centralized safety work rather than broad unrelated redesign.</li><li>□ A New Files section is justified if it proposes a security module; Dan’s run proposed <code>app/server/core/sql_security.py</code>.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-bug-build"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3.2B · Implement and verify like an engineer</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Fresh build agent</li><li>New security coverage</li><li>Human reproduction required</li></ul>
+          <p>Preserve the bug plan, open a fresh instance, and run <code>/implement &lt;bug-plan-path&gt;</code>. When it reports completion, perform the checks yourself.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Require <code>uv run pytest</code> to pass with at least one new injection-focused test file and zero regressions. Do not require Dan’s exact 52/18 counts.</li><li>Run the plan’s Steps to Reproduce through the UI. Try an injection-shaped request involving concatenation or a <code>'); DROP TABLE users;--</code>-style string. A safe result or clean error is acceptable; a schema change is not.</li><li>Confirm the data remains present after the attempt.</li><li>Run pytest yourself rather than relying on the agent transcript.</li><li>Inspect the diff for unrelated changes and compare it line by line with the plan’s stated scope.</li></ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 2B pass</h4><ul><li>□ New security tests exist and all tests pass.</li><li>□ The original risky path has a reproducible safe outcome.</li><li>□ Identifier handling and raw execution were addressed according to the reviewed plan.</li><li>□ You can explain why a keyword blocklist is not a complete defense and why SQLite’s single execute behavior does not validate careless “stacked statements” claims.</li><li>□ Human observations agree with the agent report.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-headless"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3.3 · Generate a feature plan headlessly</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15 min</li><li>Plain terminal</li><li>Do not implement</li></ul>
+          <p>From a plain terminal—not an interactive Claude Code session—run a course-repo feature request in the video’s shape. You may use the query-history request or another feature confined to the TAC-3 application.</p>
+          <div class="terminal"><div class="terminal__bar">plain terminal · planning-only authority</div><pre><code>claude -p "/feature \"&lt;course-app feature description&gt;\"" \
+  --output-format stream-json \
+  --dangerously-skip-permissions \
+  --model &lt;your-best-model&gt; \
+  --verbose \
+  &gt; feature_plan_raw_agent_logs.jsonl</code></pre></div>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Before firing, state why the permission bypass is narrowly scoped here: planning only, isolated course demo, no implementation phase.</li><li>Tail the JSONL as it grows.</li><li>Parse it line by line as JSON after completion.</li><li>Identify a system/init event and assistant/tool activity.</li><li>Review the new feature plan for User Story, three implementation phases, Testing Strategy, Acceptance Criteria, and Validation Commands.</li><li>Do not implement the feature for the required checkpoint.</li></ol>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 3 pass</h4><ul><li>□ <code>feature_plan_raw_agent_logs.jsonl</code> exists and every non-empty line parses.</li><li>□ A matching plan exists in <code>specs/</code>.</li><li>□ The plan was produced without an interactive session.</li><li>□ The plan follows <code>feature.md</code>’s exact class-specific shape.</li><li>□ You can point to the machine-readable observation boundary another workflow could consume.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-stretch"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Course-repo stretch · make the system more composable</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Optional</li><li>20–40 min</li><li>Still zero hand-typed app code</li></ul>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li><strong>Specialize.</strong> Create <code>.claude/commands/bug-frontend.md</code> inside TAC-3, restrict its relevant files to <code>app/client/**</code>, preserve bug reasoning sections, and run it against the same UI-shaped symptom as the generic planner. Diff the two generated plans.</li><li><strong>Write a HOP variant.</strong> Create <code>implement-and-review.md</code> that implements a plan, then reviews completed work against the plan and reports deviations before the normal summary.</li><li><strong>Chain the nodes.</strong> In the TAC-3 repo, create a small shell workflow that runs headless feature planning, identifies the resulting spec, and launches a second headless <code>/implement</code> only on an isolated experimental branch. This is optional because it crosses from planning into execution and requires a deliberately reviewed authority boundary.</li></ol>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-troubleshoot"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Troubleshooting matrix</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Repair upstream</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>/install</code> cannot copy the environment file</td><td>No sibling TAC-2 <code>.env</code></td><td>Use the documented <code>app/server/.env.sample</code> fallback.</td></tr><tr><td>Generated plan omits files that exist</td><td>Relevant Files or research instruction too narrow</td><td>Correct the template/plan and regenerate; do not patch implementation from memory.</td></tr><tr><td>Plan contains placeholders</td><td>Output contract not followed</td><td>Keep the replace-every-placeholder instruction and reject the plan before build.</td></tr><tr><td>Fresh implementer asks basic planning questions</td><td>The plan does not carry enough context</td><td>Improve the plan; do not rebuild context in chat.</td></tr><tr><td>Different test counts than the video</td><td>Generated implementation varies</td><td>Grade zero regressions plus meaningful new security coverage.</td></tr><tr><td>JSONL is empty or not event-shaped</td><td>Invocation/format/verbosity issue</td><td>Check quoting, <code>-p</code>, output format, verbosity, redirect, and model availability.</td></tr><tr><td>Headless run requests unsafe authority</td><td>Scope exceeds planning-only demo</td><td>Stop and redesign with bounded tools, permission controls, and isolation.</td></tr></tbody></table></section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-final-pass"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Final pass sheet</h3><div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Artifacts and behavior</h4><ul><li>□ Zero application code typed by hand.</li><li>□ At least three generated plans in <code>specs/</code>: chore, bug, feature.</li><li>□ Every implemented plan used a different fresh agent from its planner.</li><li>□ Pytest green after chore and bug work; new injection coverage exists.</li><li>□ Logging grep and stdout behavior pass.</li><li>□ Human bug reproduction leaves schema/data safe.</li><li>□ JSONL parses and corresponds to a non-interactively created feature plan.</li></ul><h4>Oral mastery</h4><ul><li>□ Prompt → plan → template → HOP → programmatic invocation.</li><li>□ Meta prompt versus HOP with lesson examples.</li><li>□ Five template parts and three class specializations.</li><li>□ Three fresh-agent reasons and four KPIs.</li><li>□ Two self-validation mechanisms plus the human check.</li></ul></div></section>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">12</span><h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery · retrieve, diagnose, compose</h2><p class="section-lead">Close the chapter and answer on paper first. A recognized phrase earns little credit unless you can use it to design or repair the TAC-3 workflow.</p>
+        <h3>Round 1 · Foundations</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Foundations mastery"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Complete the five-rung composition ladder.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>High-level prompt → plan/spec/PRD → template/meta-prompt → higher-order prompt → programmatic invocation.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Define meta prompt and HOP by their input/output behavior.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A meta prompt produces another prompt, here a plan. A HOP accepts a prompt as input, here a plan file passed to <code>/implement</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is the 749-line initial spec evidence for the lesson thesis?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is a durable natural-language instruction that carried construction of the application, showing a plan can function as a scaled executable prompt.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What output do all three planning templates share?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A reviewable plan under <code>specs/</code>.</p></details></div></section>
+        <h3>Round 2 · Trace the course artifacts</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Artifact mastery"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Name the five common parts of chore, bug, and feature templates.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Purpose; Instructions; Relevant Files; Plan Format; final section containing <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. Which sections make <code>bug.md</code> causal rather than merely procedural?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Steps to Reproduce and Root Cause Analysis, supported by Problem and Solution Statements and expected/actual behavior.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. Which sections make <code>feature.md</code> value- and acceptance-oriented?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>User Story, phased Implementation Plan, Testing Strategy, and Acceptance Criteria.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. Reconstruct <code>implement.md</code>.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Purpose; one instruction to read, think hard, and implement; Plan containing <code>$ARGUMENTS</code>; Report containing bullets and <code>git diff --stat</code>.</p></details></div></section>
+        <h3>Round 3 · Diagnose failures</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Diagnosis mastery"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. A build succeeds only when run in the same planning conversation. What failed?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The plan has a hidden conversational dependency and is not a zero-dependency reusable asset.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. A copied template searches directories that do not exist. What should change?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The template’s Relevant Files and validation seed must be rewritten for the actual repository before generating plans.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. An agent reports 20 print calls; the line inventory and disk show 19. What do you trust?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The enumerated, independently checked repository evidence: 18 in <code>server.py</code> and one in <code>main.py</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. A bug run has fewer than 52 tests but adds targeted coverage and has zero regressions. Pass or fail?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Potentially pass. The 52/18 numbers describe one generated run; grade the invariants and evidence.</p></details></div></section>
+        <h3>Round 4 · Composition and judgment</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Composition mastery"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">13. Why does a fresh agent improve diagnosability?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Its success-critical inputs are known artifacts rather than a long, ambiguous conversation, so failures can be traced to the template, plan, executor, repository, or tools.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">14. Why is JSONL more than a log?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It is a structured stream another program or agent can parse to observe state and compose the planning node with later workflow nodes.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">15. When does a specialized template become too narrow?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>When its context or prescribed process excludes real surfaces needed to explain or validate the work; outcome evidence should trigger widening or a different class boundary.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">16. Resolve “Success is Planned” without motivational language.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Success criteria, context, task order, and validation are encoded into a durable prompt produced by an improvable template and tested through fresh execution.</p></details></div></section>
+        <aside class="callout callout--why"><p class="callout__label">Scoring</p><p><strong>14–16:</strong> ready to teach and proceed. <strong>10–13:</strong> rerun the associated lab checkpoint. <strong>0–9:</strong> redraw the ladder and reproduce the chore plan/build split. A definition you cannot use to diagnose a workflow earns half credit.</p></aside>
+        <h3>Capstone teach-back</h3><div class="teach-back"><h4>Five minutes, no notes</h4><ol><li>Explain the ladder and define meta prompt and HOP with <code>/chore</code> and <code>/implement</code>.</li><li>Recite the three fresh-agent reasons and connect them to size, attempts, streak, and presence.</li><li>Show how a plan checks itself and what the human still checks.</li><li>Explain what changes and stays identical when <code>/feature</code> runs via <code>claude -p</code>.</li><li>Finish by resolving the title as a concrete system property.</li></ol><p class="fine">Expert pass: your listener can predict why the same-session shortcut and the copied-unchanged-template shortcut both fail.</p></div>
       </section>
 
-      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-template">
-        <span class="section-number">02</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Template your engineering</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A plan handles one piece of work. A template encodes how you plan an entire class of work, so the expensive reasoning can be reused and improved.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Reinventing “good planning” every Monday</h3><p>Your team agrees that bugs need root-cause analysis and features need acceptance criteria. Yet each agent session begins with a blank prompt. One bug plan includes reproduction; the next skips it. One feature plan names edge cases; the next jumps directly to components. Good outcomes depend on whoever remembers the house style that day.</p><p>The failure is not lack of knowledge. The team already knows what good looks like. It has not encoded that knowledge at the moment the agent needs it.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — amortized judgment</p><h3>Pay for the planning decision once; collect it on every run</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Observe practice</strong><code>how we solve bugs</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Encode</strong><code>.claude/commands/bug.md</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Run</strong><code>/bug "symptom"</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Review</strong><code>plan quality</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Improve</strong><code>edit upstream</code></div></div><p>The template is a reusable decision surface. It does not guarantee the plan is right. It ensures every run begins with the same expectations and leaves a visible place to improve them.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story — three classes, three templates</p><h3>The repo refuses one-format-fits-all planning</h3><p>TAC-3 ships <code>chore.md</code>, <code>bug.md</code>, and <code>feature.md</code>. All three research the repository and write into <code>specs/</code>, but they do not ask identical questions. The bug template requires steps to reproduce, a problem statement, a solution statement, and root-cause analysis. The feature template requires a user story, three implementation phases, a testing strategy, and acceptance criteria. The chore template stays lighter.</p><p>This specialization is the tactic. The filenames are not organizational decoration; each one carries an opinion about what its class of work needs before execution.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-3-success-is-planned/t0470-chore-prompt-fired.png" alt="Course editor showing install command content while a one-line slash chore request is entered" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">7:50</span>A compact invocation selects an encoded workflow. The one-line request can stay high-level because the chore template supplies the planning process.</figcaption></figure></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Tactic 03</p><h3>Template Your Engineering</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Template Your Engineering:</strong> encode engineering workflows as reusable, scalable units of agentic success.</p></aside><p>Plain English: write down your repeatable planning judgment where an agent can execute it. The object you are templating is not merely markdown style. You are templating scope discipline, research order, expected analysis, task sequencing, proof, and reporting.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — classify before prompting</p><h3>The same sentence changes when its work class changes</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Question the template must force</th><th>Typical proof</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Chore</td><td>What mechanical change must happen everywhere without behavior drift?</td><td>Inventory, grep, regression tests, observed output.</td></tr><tr><td>Bug</td><td>How is the failure reproduced, and what mechanism causes it?</td><td>Before/after repro, focused tests, root-cause trace.</td></tr><tr><td>Feature</td><td>Who benefits, what phases integrate it, and what defines done?</td><td>Acceptance criteria, unit/integration/edge tests, end-to-end behavior.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If a request says “query history panel,” the feature shape matters: user value, UI/server integration, persistence or state decisions, and acceptance criteria. If a request says “SQL injection,” the bug shape matters: reproduction and root cause precede solution. The template chooses the questions before the model chooses answers.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — classification drill</p><h3>Route six tasks before writing any prompt</h3><ol><li>Replace raw prints with logging.</li><li>A crafted query can read data outside the intended shape.</li><li>Add a query-history side panel.</li><li>Rename an internal script while preserving callers.</li><li>An upload produces an invalid table identifier.</li><li>Add a button that toggles a new panel.</li></ol><p>Label each chore, bug, or feature and state the single template section that matters most. Reasonable classifications may differ when the symptom hides a larger product decision; the point is to make the planning contract explicit.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: the universal mega-template</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> every task gets user stories, root-cause analysis, migration plans, rollout strategy, and twenty acceptance criteria. Small chores become expensive; bugs acquire irrelevant product prose; people stop trusting the generated plans.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> keep a small common spine—purpose, research, scope, output, validation—and specialize only where the class of work demands different evidence. A template creates leverage when it removes repeated thought without manufacturing ceremony.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Command templates and Agent Skills are adjacent layers</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Repo-local markdown commands remain a lightweight way to generate plans in a specific codebase. Agent Skills package broader reusable expertise with discovery metadata and supporting resources. The continuity matters more than the packaging: both make engineering judgment an explicit, inspectable asset. Use a local command for a project-specific planning entry point; use a Skill when the workflow and supporting knowledge need team-scale reuse.</p><p class="current-practice__source">Optional supplement from the extract: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU">templates-to-Skills current-practice video</a>. The chapter itself remains fully usable offline.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Template engineering retrieval questions"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What is templated besides markdown formatting?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Research order, scope, engineering analysis, task order, validation, and reporting for a class of work.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Name one section unique to the bug shape and one associated with the feature shape.</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Bug: Steps to Reproduce or Root Cause Analysis. Feature: User Story, phased Implementation Plan, Testing Strategy, or Acceptance Criteria.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Where do all three planning templates write?</p><details><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Into a markdown plan under <code>specs/</code>; none of the planning templates implements application code.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>Explain why three templates beat one “smart” prompt.</strong> Use chore, bug, and feature as evidence, and identify the repeated spine they still share.</p><p class="fine">Pass if your listener understands specialization as encoded engineering judgment, not merely different filenames.</p></div></div>
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">13</span><h2 class="section-title">TAC-3 glossary</h2><p class="section-lead">Use these terms narrowly enough that a teammate can point to their artifact in the course repository.</p>
+        <dl class="glossary"><div><dt>Plan / spec / PRD</dt><dd>A prompt scaled up for high-impact work; in TAC-3, a durable Markdown artifact under <code>specs/</code>.</dd></div><div><dt>High-level prompt</dt><dd>A deliberately detail-light statement of the desired outcome passed into a planning template.</dd></div><div><dt>Template</dt><dd>A reusable prompt file encoding how a class of work should be researched, structured, validated, and reported.</dd></div><div><dt>Template Your Engineering</dt><dd>Tactic #3: encode problem-solving practice into reusable units that humans and agents can see, use, and improve.</dd></div><div><dt>Meta prompt</dt><dd>A prompt that builds a prompt. <code>/chore</code>, <code>/bug</code>, and <code>/feature</code> produce plans.</dd></div><div><dt>Higher Order Prompt (HOP)</dt><dd>A prompt accepting another prompt as input. <code>/implement</code> consumes a plan path.</dd></div><div><dt>Plan Format</dt><dd>The exact Markdown skeleton a planning agent must fill, including validation and notes.</dd></div><div><dt><code>$ARGUMENTS</code></dt><dd>The command parameter receiving the unique request or plan path.</dd></div><div><dt>Relevant Files</dt><dd>The explicit repository search boundary encoded in each planner.</dd></div><div><dt>THINK HARD</dt><dd>A course-era information-dense reasoning trigger; today, retain the principle of intentional reasoning budget.</dd></div><div><dt>Fresh agent</dt><dd>A new instance without the preceding phase’s conversation, used to test artifact completeness and focus context.</dd></div><div><dt>Zero-dependency asset</dt><dd>A prompt, plan, or template whose success does not silently rely on prior conversation.</dd></div><div><dt>One-shot success</dt><dd>A phase completing from its declared artifacts without iterative human steering.</dd></div><div><dt>Presence KPI</dt><dd>The amount of human attendance required; the workflow aims to move presence toward zero while preserving evidence.</dd></div><div><dt>Self-validation</dt><dd>Execution of embedded checks and review of resulting work so observations can trigger correction before reporting.</dd></div><div><dt>Steps to Reproduce</dt><dd>A bug-plan section defining the observable before/after behavior a human or agent can rerun.</dd></div><div><dt>Root Cause Analysis</dt><dd>A causal account of why the bug exists across the real system, not a restatement of symptoms.</dd></div><div><dt>Programmatic form</dt><dd>Headless invocation through <code>claude -p</code> rather than an interactive terminal session.</dd></div><div><dt>stream-json / JSONL</dt><dd>A sequence of structured event objects, one per line, exposing system, assistant, and tool activity to another program.</dd></div><div><dt>AI developer workflow</dt><dd>A composed software-development lifecycle whose agentic phases can run programmatically; TAC-3 previews the planning node.</dd></div><div><dt>Staging environment for code</dt><dd>Dan’s metaphor for plans: inspect and refine the natural-language representation before expanding it into implementation.</dd></div><div><dt>System that builds the system</dt><dd>The template-and-HOP layer that produces and executes the plans used to change the application.</dd></div></dl>
       </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer"><p class="eyebrow">Volume I · Tactical Agentic Coding</p><div class="chapter-footer__nav"><a href="../index.html">← Book hub</a><span class="fine">Next: TAC-4 · AFK Agents (pending)</span></div></footer>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+  <script>
+    (() => {
+      const topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
+      const railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
+      const label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
+      const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a')];
+      const sections = links.map(link => document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href'))).filter(Boolean);
+      let pending = false;
+      const update = () => {
+        const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
+        const value = max > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / max)) : 0;
+        const percent = `${Math.round(value * 100)}%`;
+        topBar.style.width = percent; railBar.style.width = percent; label.textContent = percent;
+      };
+      const requestUpdate = () => { if (pending) return; pending = true; requestAnimationFrame(() => { update(); pending = false; }); };
+      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
+        const visible = entries.filter(entry => entry.isIntersecting).sort((a,b) => b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio)[0];
+        if (!visible) return;
+        links.forEach(link => { if (link.getAttribute('href') === `#${visible.target.id}`) link.setAttribute('aria-current','location'); else link.removeAttribute('aria-current'); });
+      }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0,.1,.5] });
+      sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section)); update();
+      window.addEventListener('scroll', requestUpdate, { passive:true }); window.addEventListener('resize', requestUpdate);
+    })();
+  </script>
+</body>
+</html>

