IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/03-success-is-planned.html:240:          <aside class="callout callout--why"><p class="callout__label">Phase boundary and safe recovery</p><p>Commit the reviewed chore spec and implementation as a checkpoint before beginning the bug phase. If a later run goes sideways, inspect <code>git status</code> first and restart from that known checkpoint in a fresh course-repo branch or fresh course clone. Do not delete files or discard unknown work to force a reset; the reproducibility lesson is to re-fire from the durable plan and known state.</p></aside>
IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/03-success-is-planned.html:252:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li>Require <code>uv run pytest</code> to pass with at least one new injection-focused test file and zero regressions. Do not require Dan’s exact 52/18 counts.</li><li>Run deterministic direct tests for the raw-query and identifier surfaces. Treat an injection-shaped UI request as supplemental because the LLM may translate the same language differently.</li><li>If you perform the UI check, confirm a safe result or clean error and verify the data/schema remains present after the attempt.</li><li>Run pytest yourself rather than relying on the agent transcript.</li><li>Inspect the diff for unrelated changes and compare it line by line with the plan’s stated scope.</li></ol>
IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/03-success-is-planned.html:253:          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Checkpoint 2B pass</h4><ul><li>□ At least one new injection-focused test file exists and all tests pass.</li><li>□ The original risky paths have deterministic reproducible safe outcomes.</li><li>□ Identifier handling and raw execution were addressed according to the reviewed plan.</li><li>□ You can explain why a keyword blocklist is not a complete defense and why SQLite’s single execute behavior does not validate careless “stacked statements” claims.</li><li>□ Human observations agree with the agent report.</li></ul></div>
IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/03-success-is-planned.html:269:          <ol class="lab-box__steps"><li><strong>Specialize.</strong> Create <code>.claude/commands/bug-frontend.md</code> inside TAC-3, restrict its relevant files to <code>app/client/**</code>, preserve bug reasoning sections, and run it against the same UI-shaped symptom as the generic planner. Diff the two generated plans.</li><li><strong>Write and run a HOP variant.</strong> Create <code>implement-and-review.md</code> that implements a plan, then reviews completed work against the plan and reports deviations before the normal summary. Run it on a fresh course-repo branch against the chore plan and compare its report with Checkpoint 1B.</li><li><strong>Chain the nodes.</strong> In the TAC-3 repo, create a small shell workflow that runs headless feature planning, identifies the resulting spec, and launches a second headless <code>/implement</code> only on an isolated experimental branch. This is optional because it crosses from planning into execution and requires a deliberately reviewed authority boundary.</li></ol>
